e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29681:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/wiringcliff/DERIVED_20260909.txt"
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29771:     "note": "SECOND REVISION, on 7 of 9 seats (opus5 now landed; agy not fired).  THE FIRST REVISION, ONE ENTRY ABOVE, IS SUPERSEDED IN THREE PLACES AND SAYS SO HERE RATHER THAN BEING EDITED: it kept fbpending design-blocked on a cap derivation (the cap is the WRONG INSTRUMENT -- the bound is already gated by an equality); it kept four WDC files in one round (they are 3 + 1, and _sig's media blocker is MEASURED FALSE); and it counted six obligations (there are SEVEN, and my own deriver printed the seventh at check_probe_wiring.py:1328 and I read past it -- the third correlate-not-the-property error of the day, all mine).  Every Opus claim relied on here was re-verified in the tree before this was written: gate_offline.sh:1084/:1165, check_probe_wiring.py:1328, sig_probe.py:75, gate_asan_sweep.sh:99.  DISPOSITION, REV 2.  (1) TWO rounds, not one: `wdcaltoffleg' owns the three altoffset files (one module, one rig, one media family; the parent's `ok' first; _4bit's single-leg form is an ORACLE CHOICE and says so; _sig owes an oracle REDESIGN, not a bolted-on contract); `wdcsingleleg' is RETITLED to addtoinbuf alone, with the poisoned image and the endianness axis as its dependencies.  (2) THREE things are decidable TODAY and all three go in the wiringcliff commit after #516: hpcmips WIRED into gate_asan_sweep.sh BEFORE the :99 gate_skip, additive rows named, its EXEMPT entry DELETED in the same edit; fbpending WIRED with NO CAP as an apparatus-rot row named for what it tests, its EXEMPT entry deleted in the same edit, and the SM_EXEMPT fbpending_bound lane discharged the same way (it is the cheapest item in the set); the checker's FALSE comment at :1333-1334 and _sig's FALSE reason string corrected as L4s.  (3) The three altoffset entries re-dated naming `wdcaltoffleg'; addtoinbuf re-dated naming `wdcsingleleg'; each DELETED in the same edit as its wiring when its round ships.  (4) NEW ROW `witnessclassdoor' (med): the CLASS_WITNESS field is an undated door for un-gated detectors; the fix is a class rule with a dated cutoff, like every checker change here -- its own round, not a rider.  On the record: the ledger's EIGHT was an expired measurement (8 -> 7 -> 6), not a transcription; DERIVED section 9 corrects section 1."
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29789:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/wiringcliff/codex_fbpending_20260910.err",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29791:     "note": "GPT-6 Astra @ max, file-reading (brief_fbpending_20260910.md); no refusal; read-only python checks of the checker's scanner against shell text in memory.  CORRECTS THREE PREMISES OF THE BRIEF: (1) the dated EXEMPT entry is on `fbpending_drain_probe.py' (REPORT-ONLY, EXPECT_CAP), NOT on fbpending_witness.py -- wiring the witness does not discharge it, keep it; (2) no candidate gate literally satisfies `free-runs guests with no wall-clock oracle': gate_hygiene runs no guest, gate_offline runs differentials, gate_ab runs long guests but keeps its stall/backstop oracles -- host it in gate_ab.sh SERIALIZED AFTER all existing A/B work, and convert gate_ab.sh:66's `need_exec' (which exits via gate_skip and would make the appended witness vanish whenever a baseline binary is missing) into a conditional with a `degrade' else-arm; (3) the witness becomes CLASS_DETECTOR once gated (`#442 post-fix apparatus: controls, coverage, no clock freeze'), or the checker reports GATED WITNESS.  Supplied the full section text: the gate creates the four-byte little-endian ARM NOP stub itself (neither the gate nor the witness has a fixture), runs `python3 \"$HERE/fbpending_witness.py\" \"$ROOT/build/gxemul\" \"$FBPENDING_STUB\"', and checks exit status, >= 12 control rows, CONTROL=OK, COVERAGE=OK (prefix match -- the line carries an explanatory suffix), SYMPTOM=ABSENT and VERDICT=PASS as separate rows so `not run long enough' and `broken' cannot render alike; ~2 min budget, source-derived.  The selfmutant lane: a `selfmutant_one' line for diff_fbpending_bound with FBBOUND_PASS/FAIL as the verdict contract, plus replacing gate_offline.sh:1263's missing-object `note' with a `degrade' (otherwise the section can omit the value gate while merely printing a note); the helper runs its binary without arguments so R2 stays skipped while R3/R4 exercise the mutant; `exempt_expired' is strict `<', so a 09-20 exemption first reds on 09-21 (as DERIVED_20260909 said).  Verified the checker derives gate scripts from run.sh's GATES array, recognises a python interpreter at command position with `$HERE' paths, excludes comments and heredocs, and would find the quoted invocation.  Could not verify the NO-CAP ruling itself (took it from the packet).  These edits are the wiringcliff commit's fbpending half."
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29799:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/wiringcliff/codex_plan_20260910.txt",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29810:     "note": "*** MEASURED 2026-09-17 BY THE OPUS 5 SEAT, ON A BATCH THAT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT WIRING -- AND IT IS THE SHARPEST ANSWER IN THAT BATCH TO \"what is the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes?\" ***  For four of the nine rows reviewed, **the answer is THE ENTIRE FIX, REVERTED, and the row that stays GREEN is every row in the full battery.**     lca_confbus_probe.py      NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (lcashiftub)\n    lca_confwidth_probe.py    NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (lcaconfwidth, MED)\n    alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py  NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (alphapfnoffset, **HIGH**)\n    alpha_harness_probe.py    NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (alphaswpctxptbr, **HIGH**)\n  -- against the wired controls in the same batch: sh_iprbcd_probe.py, sgi_re_rgb_probe.py, gdrom_default_probe.py and diff_wdc_identify.c all ARE referenced.  `run.sh` runs an explicit `GATES=(...)` list and the four appear nowhere outside `regress/` and the wiring checker itself. *** TWO OF THE FOUR ARE HIGH-SEVERITY ROWS.  Delete `| (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1))` from `memory_alpha.c` and a HIGH page-walk defect is back with a CLEAN BATTERY. *** **THE SEAT EXPLICITLY DECLINED TO CLAIM THIS AS NEW, which is the right call and is why it lands here rather than as a fresh row:** `check_probe_wiring.py` already classifies all four as `CLASS_WITNESS` -- correctly, by the ladder's witness/detector timing line -- and its own entry for `lca_confbus_probe.py` says *\"but this probe RUNS IN NO GATE, so the coverage exists and is never executed: wiringcliff\"*. *** BUT THAT CHECKER IS UNCOMMITTED, so **nothing in any committed tree reports this today.** ***  The four closures are HONEST -- their `witness:` says `probe:`, which is exactly what it is -- and what is absent is STANDING DEFENCE.  The seat's disposition, adopted: this belongs in each round record as a DISCLOSED RESIDUAL on those four rows, not only as one filed id, because a reader of `alphapfnoffset` alone cannot see it."
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34820:   "state_note": "\n\n*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER\nREPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***\n\n    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes\n    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\n    tokens used:  108,432\n\n**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and\nenumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a\nseat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal\ntext.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the\ncheck printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns\nthe same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project\nnamed after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a\nvariable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task\nCOMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has\nthree prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).\n\n> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6\n> on an empty answer with neither, and `rc` otherwise.**  A refused seat can no longer render as a\n> completed one.\n\n**THE TRIGGER, added to the recorded set.**  The brief asked, of a kill diagnostic, *\"say whether a\n64-bit value exists that trips the guard and prints as something the guard would accept -- give the\ndiscriminating value where one exists.\"*  That is a diagnostic-correctness question, but its SHAPE\nis *find the input that defeats the check*, and it sits beside an enumeration of `exit(1)` sites.\n**Chain-shaped content, whatever the question form** -- the trigger this project already recorded,\nmet again.\n\n**ROUTED TO GROK**, which the carrier records as the working path for a refused brief and which has\nanswered three of them, finding something material in each.\n\n*** GROK CENSUS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_census_20260907.txt`), after\ncodex refused the brief.  IT CORRECTED MY COUNT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND NARROWED THE FIX TO TWO\nLINES. ***\n\n**MY CENSUS MISSED A SPELLING.**  I grepped `(int)idata`; the tree also writes **`(int) idata`**\nwith a space.  Real total **309 sites in 70 files**, not 226 -- and 309 itself includes\nCOMMENTED-OUT casts (`dev_clmpcc.c:125`, `dev_dc7085.c:236`, `dev_dec21143.c:914/1072`), so the live\nfigure is lower again.\n\n**AND MY PROXIMITY SET WAS SHORT: 18 live sites within +/-3 of an `exit(1)`, not 15.**  The three I\nmissed are `g2` CHAN_ENABLE, `g2` XFER_ENABLE and the `osiop` default -- a blank line inside the\nwindow is still inside the window.  grok also priced the string-vs-call distinction: a scanner that\ncounted comment text would report **30** (18 live + 2 commented-out `exit(1)` + 10 `dev_pvr.c`\n*\"don't exit(1)\"* comments).\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT SETS THE DISPOSITION, and it is the opposite of a tree-wide class: ***\n\n    (uint64_t)idata  IS next to a live exit(1) FIVE times, ALL IN dev_lca.c\n        225/226  TB_ENA      240/241  W_BASE0     255/256  W_MASK0\n        279/280  W_BASE1     294/295  W_MASK1\n    (uint32_t)idata  13 sites, NONE within +/-3 of a live exit(1)\n    (long)idata       1 site, not near one\n    (int32_t) / (unsigned int) idata   0\n\n**Those five LCA guards already print the FULL 64-bit value.**  So `dev_lca.c` is mostly correct\nalready, and the narrowing print survives at exactly **two** sites in it -- `:205` (IOC_CONF, the\nexemplar) and `:313`.  **The repair is two lines in one file, matching the idiom its own siblings\ntwelve lines away already use.**  That is a transferred warrant from inside the same function, which\nis the strongest kind this project recognises, and it makes the sibling-disposition check answer FOR\na local fix here -- the opposite of how it answered for [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n\n**Also found: the filed PVR 'sharpest instance' is ALREADY DE-FATALISED by `#166`** -- the fourth\nalready-fixed claim surfaced today.\n\n> **ORDERING vs [[lcabadval]]:** that row's witness must match `fatal()` TEXT rather than exit status\n> (because the interrupt stubs kill with the same status), and IOC_CONF is one of the arms it would\n> read.  With the repair confined to two lines whose siblings already do it right, taking this first\n> is cheap and removes the oracle's one unsound arm.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ESTABLISHED BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- so a rung-3 witness is a construction\nproblem rather than an open question. ***\n\n    alpha_lcareg.h      LCA_IOC_BASE 0x180000000 ; LCA_IOC_CONF = BASE + 0x020 = 0x180000020\n    dev_lca.c :359-361  memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000,\n                        dev_lca_ioc_access, ..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)   <- NO length constraint\n    dev_lca.c :188      idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)     <- takes the full width\n    machine_alpha.c :61 device_add(machine, \"lca\") -- inside `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:` ONLY\n\n**And the technique is already proven on that exact machine.**  `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`\nran today on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` at **7/9**, with `live`, `exec` and `palgate`\ngreen; it builds K0SEG addresses as `0xfffffc0000000000 | x` and K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffff`, so `k0(0x180000020)` reaches the register with no page tables.\n\n**The 64-bit question is answered too:** Alpha `STQ` is opcode `0x2d` with `loadstore_type = 3`\n(quadword) -- established in the grok harness review against `cpu_alpha_instr.c` -- so a guest `stq`\ndelivers `len = 8` and `idata` holds bit 32.  **The discriminating store is therefore\n`0x100000000` to `k0(0x180000020)`: the guard `idata & ~1` fires, and `(int)idata` prints `0x0` --\na value that same guard ACCEPTS.**\n\n> **THE CONTROL THIS WITNESS OWES, and it is the device form of the trap that nearly voided the\n> Alpha harness:** if `k0(0x180000020)` does NOT land on the LCA window, the store goes to unmapped\n> space and **nothing happens at all** -- no message, no exit -- which is indistinguishable from\n> *the guard did not fire*.  So the probe needs a positive control proving the store reached\n> `dev_lca`: a legal `0x1` write that SURVIVES, and/or a read-back of `d->ioc_conf` through\n> `LCA_IOC_CONF`.  **That question is out to the grok seat rather than settled here**, because\n> whether either is sufficient alone is exactly the judgement this project keeps getting wrong.\n\n*Design brief: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/WITNESS_brief.md`.  Sent to grok rather than codex --\ncodex refused the sibling census brief on its content filter, and the shape 'find the value that\ndefeats the check' is the recorded trigger even when the subject is a printf format specifier.*\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN, 2026-09-07 (`grok_witness2_20260907.txt`).  VERDICT: CONSTRUCTIBLE as a\nrung-3 witness on stock `alphabook1`.  It corrected FOUR of my premises, one of which was a wrong\nrecord in this row's own OUTSTANDING_BUGS entry. ***\n\n**1. THE RECOMMENDED FIX WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS ITS WARRANT.**  The entry said `0x%llx` with a\n`(long long)` cast, *\"the file's own idiom elsewhere\"*.  `dev_lca.c` uses **`PRIx64` five times and\n`%llx` never**, and the siblings **KEEP** the cast as `(uint64_t)idata`.  My design brief compounded\nit by saying to DROP the cast -- a third wrong form.  Corrected in place.\n\n**2. `load_const` CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.**  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants; the\nK0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  **A probe using `load_const` for\neither is not this witness.**  The working bootstrap is the one the Alpha harness already uses:\n`put d` the 64-bit words into RAM below 64 MB, then guest `ldq` them while `ptbr` is still 0.\n\n**3. THE MISS MODE IS NOT ONLY 'UNMAPPED -> NOTHING'.**  Offset 0 is `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE), which\n`break`s with no kill -- so a store to `k0(0x180000000)` is **silent while INSIDE the LCA window**.\nUnmapped and HAE both read back 0, which is why the control has to distinguish them.\n\n**4. TWO ROUTES THAT LOOK LIKE CONTROLS AND ARE NOT:**\n  * **debugger `dump` cannot read the register.**  `dump` calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and\n    LCA is `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is **SKIPPED** and\n    the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer -- *the unmapped signature, produced by a hit.*\n  * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung-3.**  `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with\n    `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it DOES enter the handler and `exit(1)` from the\n    debugger -- a real kill, by no guest instruction.\n\n> **THE CONTROL THAT WORKS: a guest `stq` of `0x1` followed by a guest `ldq`, same decode, BEFORE\n> the killing store.**  It is the analogue of the Alpha harness's `swpctx mapped` row.  Neither it\n> nor the kill text is sufficient alone -- the round-trip proves the window is THIS arm and not HAE\n> or unmapped; the text `IOC_CONF: 0x0` proves the arm fired and that a 32-bit zero store could not\n> have produced that pair.\n\n**Q3 CONFIRMED IN DETAIL, and it hardens S2:** `stq` is opcode `0x2d` -> `loadstore_type 3` -> `LS_Q`;\n`host_store` is NULL for a device page, so it falls to `LS_GENERIC_N` and calls `memory_rw(..., 8,\nMEM_WRITE)`; the memblock granule is 1 MB so `0x20+8` crosses nothing; Alpha is little-endian and\n`memory_readmax64` rebuilds the value.  **And the guard's own arithmetic is load-bearing:** `~1` is a\nsigned `int` (-2) converting to `0xfffffffffffffffe`, **so it sees bit 32 -- `~1U` would NOT, and\nthat is a different mutant worth building.**  The same function already compares against\n`0x300800000ULL`, so 64-bit values are this handler's native width.\n\n**One honest weakening:** `(int)0x100000000 == 0` is IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED, not guaranteed by C99.\nGCC/Clang truncate, so the witness holds on this build -- but the row should not claim a language\nguarantee.\n\n*** AND THE 309 FIGURE WAS ALREADY IN THIS ROW'S OWN ENTRY, DATED 2026-08-31. ***  I ran a census\ntoday, got 226, treated it as new, and was corrected to 309 by a seat -- while\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` had recorded **\"309 casts across 70 device files\"** five days earlier, with\nits deriver.  **The unread-review class, in the file I was writing about.**  (Its companion figure,\n45 host-kill sites, is not in conflict with today's 18: the two used different window sizes, and\nneither states one, which is the reason they cannot be reconciled.)\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 4/6. ***\n\n    live          alive   alive          ok    the control session survives the legal write\n    ioc rt        0x1     0x1            ok    guest stq 0x1 then guest ldq, SAME decode\n    kill fired    yes     yes            ok    the IOC_CONF arm announced -- not HAE, not unmapped\n    kill width    0x0     0x100000000    FAIL  the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed\n    kill notzero  0x0     NOT 0x0        FAIL  0x0 is a value this guard ACCEPTS\n    died          died    died           ok    exit(1) really ran\n\nAll four of grok's design corrections are implemented and each is a comment in the probe: the\n64-bit operands are planted with `put d` and loaded with `ldq` (`load_const` cannot build them);\nthe read-back is a guest `ldq` (debugger `dump` would skip a `DM_DEFAULT` handler and hand back the\npre-zeroed buffer -- the unmapped signature from a hit); the killing store is a guest `stq` (a\n`put d` would kill from the debugger and not be rung-3); and `ioc rt` distinguishes the real arm\nfrom HAE at offset 0, which `break`s silently INSIDE the window.\n\n*** AND MY FIRST DRAFT HAD THE ORACLE POLARITY BACKWARDS -- it scored 6/6 on the committed build. ***\nIt asserted the DEFECT as the passing state, so it read as success and would have gone RED the\nmoment the bug was fixed.  **The ladder requires a witness to show the symptom pre-fix and go green\npost-fix WITH THE SAME SCRIPT**, which is clause (i).  The sibling `alpha_harness_probe.py` had it\nright (7/9) and this one did not, in the same tree, on the same day.\n\n> *A witness that passes on the committed build is a detector-of-the-defect wearing a witness's\n> clothes.  The tell is cheap and I nearly missed it: **ask what the row does after the fix.**  If\n> the answer is 'goes red', the polarity is inverted whatever the score says.*\n\n**The repair is now fully specified and unblocks [[lcabadval]]:** `0x%\" PRIx64` with\n`(uint64_t)idata`, copying the five sibling arms verbatim; no gate, hygiene grep or probe in\n`regress/` matches this message's text, so changing it turns nothing else red.\n\n*** `#506` APPLIED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  THE ROUND IS COMPLETE AND HELD FOR THE 9th. ***\n\n    HEAD  4/6   probe_head_20260907.out   `kill width' 0x0,          `kill notzero' 0x0\n    fix   6/6   probe_fix_20260907.out    `kill width' 0x100000000,  `kill notzero' wider\n\n**Same script, red before and green after** -- ladder clause (i) discharged by the pair rather than\nasserted.  Both sites repaired: `:205` (IOC_CONF, the sharp instance -- guard on the VALUE) and\n`:319` (the `default:` arm, weaker -- guard on the OFFSET, so its narrowed value is wrong without\nbeing one the guard accepts).\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt with the\ntwo-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold\nstands, and this round now waits as FINISHED WORK rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** THE WITNESS HAD A ROW THAT COULD NOT FAIL, AND A PASS-2 REVIEW OF THE ROUND FOUND IT.\nREPAIRED AND CONTROLLED, 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe rows tested `\"IOC_CONF: 0x100000000\" in text` -- a SUBSTRING -- and manufactured the displayed\n`got` from the boolean.  codex: *\"Append one literal 0 before the diagnostic's newline.  The message\nbecomes 0x1000000000, yet `kill width` still reports a green 0x100000000.\"*  `zero` had the mirror\nflaw (`0x0abc` contains `0x0`).\n\n> **That is the check-that-cannot-fail shape INSIDE THE WITNESS FOR THE DEFECT IT EXISTS TO PIN** --\n> and it survived my own review, a design review, and the run.  It was found only by asking a seat\n> what edit keeps the witness green.\n\n**REPAIRED** to parse the number and compare integers, reporting the value actually found.\n**CONTROLLED in both directions:**\n\n    fixed build, repaired oracle          6/6      kill width 0x100000000\n    codex's escape (TRAILING 0)           5/6      kill width 0x1000000000  RED\n\n*** AND MY FIRST CONTROL TESTED THE WRONG MUTANT, which is worth recording because it would have\nbeen a false proof. ***  I applied the extra `0` BEFORE the value (`0x0%\" PRIx64`), giving\n`0x0100000000` -- **numerically identical to `0x100000000`**, so the row passed correctly and proved\nnothing.  That is the carrier's own *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT* rule, case 1: the mutant\nis mis-built.  **The leading-zero form is a genuine EQUIVALENT mutant** and is recorded as such\nrather than as an escape.  The trailing-zero form is codex's, and it reds.\n\n*Artifacts: `probe_fix_newora_20260907.out`, `probe_mutant_trailzero_20260907.out`, and the\nmis-built `probe_mutant_extrazero_20260907.out` kept as the record of the equivalence.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/devices/dev_lca.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/devices/dev_lca.c\n                                   contains the `#506' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*  #506.  Widened the IOC_CONF and default-arm diagnostics from `(int)idata'/`%x' to PRIx64 with (uint64_t)idata -- 2 PRIx64 sites VERIFIED LIVE AT HEAD.  *** OVERTAKEN AT THE FOUNDING SITE BY #518, AND A FUTURE READER MUST NOT MISREAD THAT: #518 DELETED the host kill this witness was measuring.  IOC_CONF now stores `idata & 0x3ULL' and announces bit 1; the surviving #506 tag is on the DECLINED DEFAULT-ARM PRINT, not on a death.  DO NOT re-run the 2026-09-07 kill-width script and read a green as `the defect is back' -- the death is gone, and the original 4/6 kill-width oracle is STALE AGAINST HEAD. ***  The product hunk on the default arm is still live, so this is a normal close with that note, not an OVERTAKEN closure needing the three conditions.  NO KILL TABLE, CLASS_WITNESS, ungated. CLOSED 2026-09-17 on the grok triage (regress/artifacts/<row>/ and _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_inwork.txt), which REFUTED THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISES: I asked about four rows with `commits` EMPTY and `sev: None`.  BOTH WERE MY QUERY BUGS -- I read `r.get(\"severity\")` when the field is `sev` (a wrong key and an absent value render IDENTICALLY), and I never queried `commits` at all, carrying `no commit yet` from a table that was true on 2026-09-07 and stale from 2026-09-08.  All four have SHAs; all four have severities.  The only real finding was that `state` was left `in work` after attribution was restored -- a lingering record, which is the resume-file rule applied to the ledger. NOT COMBINABLE.  The reopening rule was applied pairwise and every pair fails it: different files, different semantic classes, different detectors and oracles.  The shared `cpu_alpha_palcode.c, memory_alpha.c` file column was a CORRELATE -- the seat-audit derived it from the COMMIT's first src/ paths, and 7a41b3f is titled \"#505 #506 #507 #508\" and touches ~190 files.  `sgirergbroundtrip` is the control: same SHA, same bogus file column, an SGI RGB defect in dev_sgi_re.c, already closed as its own round.  More witnesses make more ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *\"I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet\"* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open \"on packet-only review\", which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file's last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *\"row 9's 'not applied' and 'unproven' descriptions are stale locally\"* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *\"an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.\"* *** THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *\"named and deliberately not applied\"*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5's LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *\"'#481's rows' is insufficient\"*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek's HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *\"edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.\"*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *\"combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does\"* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *\"these are coverage predictions, not newly run mutants.\"*  *** SEAT FAILURES ON THE 2026-09-07 PANELS, RECORDED BY NAME RATHER THAN LEFT AS BLANKS. ***  The kimi CLI WALLED on all four (350 bytes each -- echo plus quota notice, nothing to read), and `seat_verdicts.py` named every one correctly as `*** WALLED ***`, which is the detector working where the answer carries no verdict text.  On `panel_20260907_104814` the glm seat returned **120,209 bytes and NO ANSWER** -- the thinking-burn class, *\"spent its whole budget on hidden reasoning\"*, also named correctly.  **agy was NOT CREATED on any of the four**, which predates the 2026-09-15 fire-agy-every-panel directive. *A walled seat, a burned seat and a deliberately-unfired seat are three different things, and none of them is agreement.*  *** RE-OPENED 2026-09-17: THE WITNESS PROBE SCORES **2/6 AT HEAD**, MEASURED BY THE FABLE SEAT AND PRESERVED. ***  `LCACONF_RESULT=2/6` with `LCACONF_DONE` -- three rows fail because `#518` replaced the IOC_CONF kill with a DECLINE: `kill width` **absent** (wanted `0x100000000`), `kill notzero` **absent**, and the `died` control **alive** where it wants `died`.  The artifact is at `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/head_20260917/`. **THE FIX IS SOUND; THE CLOSURE'S DETECTOR CLAIM IS FALSE AT HEAD** -- which is the distinction that decides the disposition.  The fix changed TWO diagnostic sites and the witness exercises only IOC_CONF; **the only SURVIVING site, the `(uint64_t)idata` print, has no row at all** -- no channel, a clause-(b) gap knowable by reading. *** CODEX REACHED THE SAME VERDICT INDEPENDENTLY AND FROM A DIFFERENT DIRECTION *** -- *\"the fix changed two diagnostic sites, but its witness exercises only IOC_CONF; reinstating narrowing at the default site is invisible\"* -- and I had recorded codex as CLOSE on this row because I captured the brief's heading instead of its verdict. *** AND A TRAP FOR WHOEVER TAKES `wiringcliff`: WIRING THIS PROBE AS-IS WOULD PRODUCE A PHANTOM RED. ***  The remedy here is RE-PIN OR RETIRE, not wire -- the probe's header and `check_probe_wiring.py`'s manifest line both describe the DELETED IOC_CONF kill in the present tense.  *The oracle-correction rule governs the re-pin: re-pin first, measure the committed build red under the new expectations, then change anything.*  *** L4 -- I RECORDED THE BRIEF'S OWN HEADINGS AS CODEX'S ANSWER ON ALL NINE ROWS.  Found by the Fable seat, corrected 2026-09-17. ***  My extraction matched the first line containing the row id and a word from `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` -- and **`CLOSE` matched the word `closed` inside the brief's own heading**, which codex echoes.  So nine cells read `### N. <id> (SEV, closed) -- <title>`: the packet, handed back as the seat's verdict. *** IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW AND FABLE SAID WHY: \"an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.\" ***  The blank that would have been caught was filled with something that looks like an answer. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* Codex's real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY 'SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS' ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET'S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  My own brief's S0 told the seats *\"an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction\"* -- which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, *discharged by naming the file* -- row 2's condition (c), row 8's figures, deepseek's rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *\"the file-reading seats verified it\" is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5's arithmetic, row 5's unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** glm's *\"nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire\"* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** **THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex's row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into \"the file-reading seats said CLOSE\".",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40042:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) and verified:\n\n    for (dev = 0; dev < 21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\n0x800 is a plain int (2^11).  At dev == 20 the expression is 2^31, which overflows a signed 32-bit int --\n**undefined behaviour** in C99, not merely implementation-defined.\n\n**The irony worth recording: this loop is the file's own PRECEDENT for graceful degradation.**  Its\n`dev >= 21' fallback has a commented-out fatal()/exit(1) and executes `dev = 0;' instead -- the\ndelete-the-check-and-substitute-a-safe-default disposition that [[lcabadval]] cites as a transferable\nin-tree warrant.  **The safe path contains UB.**\n\nLOW: `0x800U << dev' fixes it, and on every compiler this project targets the emitted code is already what\nwas intended.  Recorded because a UB shift is exactly what a sanitiser build exists to catch, and\nbuild-asan/ is kept clean -- **so the first question is whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not,\nwhy not.**\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n**THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED WITNESS** and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) is gradeable.\nThe repair is COMPLETE for this site: `0x800U` makes the operand unsigned, so `2048U << 20` is\n`0x80000000U`, well inside `unsigned int`, and the `tag &` comparison is unaffected because `tag` is\nalready a wider unsigned quantity.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS LOW and that is deliberate**: the overflow is UB by the language, the emitted code\non every compiler this project targets is what was intended, and no wrong guest-visible value has\nbeen measured.  What changed is that it is now WITNESSED rather than argued.\n\n*** THE FIX IS DESIGNED AND EXECUTABLE.  Written out here so the next session can run it rather than\nre-derive it -- the round is small and every input is now in hand. ***\n\n**THE EDIT, one token:**\n\n    -\tif (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n    +\tif (tag & (0x800U << dev))\t/*  #NNN  */\n\n`0x800U` makes the operand `unsigned int`, so `2048U << 20` is `0x80000000U` -- well inside\n`unsigned int` -- and no other behaviour moves: `tag` is already a wider unsigned quantity, so the\n`&` and the truth value are unchanged at every one of the 21 iterations.  **The emitted code on\nevery compiler this project targets is what was already intended**, which is why the severity is LOW\neven though the language calls it undefined.\n\n*** THE TEST-FIRST ORDER IS ALREADY SATISFIED, WHICH IS UNUSUAL AND WORTH NOTING. ***  The witness\nexists and is RED on the committed build: running `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` against\n`build-asan/gxemul` emits\n\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n\nSo the reproduce-before-you-edit step is DONE and preserved\n(`regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`).  The post-fix half is: the same command,\nsame binary path, and **the diagnostic must be ABSENT**.  Same script, opposite outcome -- which is\nthe ladder's clause (i) exactly.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND OWES, and the one real decision in it:**\n\nThe two-direction control above is a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL, and this project has that filed as its own\nround ([[ubsanshiftlane]]) because it is a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE -- a second binary and a STDERR\noracle rather than a value oracle.  **So the honest choice is:**\n\n* **(a) ship the fix with the UBSAN differential as its detector**, which means building at least a\n  minimal version of the lane in this round -- and a lane added inside a fix round is a lane nobody\n  reviewed, which is the exact objection `ubsanshiftlane` was filed to avoid; or\n* **(b) ship the fix with a SOURCE PIN** -- a check asserting the expression is `0x800U` -- and\n  leave the differential to its own round.  `#510`'s own product comments already name a source pin\n  as the legitimate alternative to UBSAN for precisely this class (\"No row can kill a `1U' -> `1'\n  regression; UBSAN or a source pin can\").\n\n**(b) is in scope and (a) is not**, on this project's own stopping rule: the fix is a one-token\ncorrectness repair, and expanding it into a new measurement lane is the scope creep the rule exists\nto refuse.  *Recorded as a reasoned choice rather than a preference, so a reviewer can disagree with\nthe reason.*\n\n**COST: a build.**  The edit must propagate to all four trees and `build-asan` must be REBUILT for\nthe post-fix measurement, which is the only expensive part and is why this was not executed the\nmoment it was designed -- a rebuild must not overlap a running seat.\n\n**AND THE POST-FIX RUN IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX.**  If the diagnostic does NOT\ndisappear, the repair is wrong or the site is not the one being reported, and that is a finding\nabout the measurement rather than a formality.  Predict-then-measure: it should vanish, and nothing\nelse in the probe's four rows should move (3/4 before, 3/4 after -- the `bus kill` row is a\ndifferent defect and stays RED).\n\n*** FIXED AND MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, 2026-09-07, as `#511`. ***\n\n    HEAD    1 diagnostic     ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw\n    FIXED   0 diagnostics    ubsan_FIXED_20260907.raw\n    control  build/ scores 3/4 before AND after, same three rows green, `bus kill' still RED\n             (a different defect) -- NOTHING ELSE MOVED\n\n**The pre-registered prediction held exactly**: the diagnostic vanished and no other row moved.  All\nfour trees cleared two-part after the propagate (`est/`: source tree, part 2 N/A).\n\n**The detector is the differential itself and it is NOT GATE-WIRED**, which the CHANGELOG block says\nin those words.  A source pin was considered and rejected: it would test the TEXT while this round\nhas the BEHAVIOUR.  Gate-wiring is [[ubsanshiftlane]], deliberately separate.\n\n*** THE SIBLING QUESTION IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE PANEL ANSWERS IT, AND THE RESULT IS A CLEAN\nABSENCE -- RECORDED WITH ITS WARRANT SO IT IS FALSIFIABLE. ***\n\n`#511`'s own pass-2 brief asks (Q5) whether a sibling of this shape was missed.  Censused:\n\n    signed-constant  <<  VARIABLE, tree-wide                       317 sites\n    of those, literal > 1 AND headroom <= 24                          2 sites\n\n**317 IS A CORRELATE, NOT THE CLASS.**  `1 << x` with 31 places of headroom is the ordinary bitmask\nidiom in C and is a defect only if `x` can reach 31.  The `#511` SHAPE is distinctive: a literal\nLARGER than 1 (so little headroom) shifted by a variable that a loop can drive to the overflow point.\nFiltering on that leaves TWO:\n\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:772   tmp &= 0x0fff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 20\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:774   tmp &= 0xffff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 16\n\n**BOTH ARE SAFE, and the warrant is a compile-time constant:**\n\n    src/include/cop0.h:85    #define PAGEMASK_SHIFT   13\n    0x0fff << 13 = 0x01ffe000       0xffff << 13 = 0x1fffe000       INT_MAX = 0x7fffffff\n\n`PAGEMASK_SHIFT` is a MACRO, not a runtime variable, so the compiler folds the whole expression and\nthere is no shift to overflow.  **My regex matched an identifier and could not tell a macro from a\nvariable** -- which is why the two hits needed reading rather than counting.\n\n> **[THIS ABSENCE RECORD IS FALSE.  See the correction at the end of this note -- the grok pass-2\n> seat found TWO siblings my filter had hidden.]**  ~~no other site in the tree has a signed literal > 1 shifted by a value that can reach 2^31.~~  THE WARRANT IS\n> `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` AND ITS BEING A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT.**  If either changes -- the value\n> raised past 15, or the macro replaced by a variable -- the two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites become\n> live and this record expires.\n\n*A near-miss worth keeping: my first attempt grepped `define PAGEMASK_SHIFT` in `src/` and returned\nNOTHING, and I had already computed the arithmetic assuming 13.  Zero hits is not evidence, and the\nnumber I was about to rely on was a guess.  It IS 13 -- found in `src/include/cop0.h` on a broader\nsearch -- but the conclusion was reached before the fact was.*\n\n*** THE ABSENCE RECORD ABOVE IS FALSE, AND MY OWN FILTER IS WHAT MADE IT SO.  The grok pass-2 seat\nfound TWO SIBLINGS, both verified here. ***\n\ngrok's verdict on `#511` closes with: *\"Two other PCI IDSEL scanners still evaluate `1 << 31`.\"*\nVerified by reading:\n\n    dev_uninorth.c:70   for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)\n                 :71       if (idata & (1 << dev))            <- dev REACHES 31\n    dev_v3.c:164        for (dev=24; dev<32; dev++)\n            :165           if (relative_addr & (1 << dev))    <- dev REACHES 31\n\n    1 << 31 = 0x80000000 = 2147483648    INT_MAX = 2147483647    OVERFLOWS\n\n**Both are the IDENTICAL SHAPE to `#511`** -- a signed literal shifted by a loop variable the loop\nbound drives to the overflow point -- and both loops explicitly run to `dev < 32`, so 31 is reached\nwhenever no earlier bit matches.\n\n*** THE FILTER THAT HID THEM WAS MINE, AND IT WAS EXACTLY BACKWARDS. ***  My census excluded\n`if v <= 1: continue`, reasoning that ``1 << x`` is the ordinary bitmask idiom in C and only a\ndefect if `x` reaches 31.  **That reasoning is right and the exclusion is wrong**: `1` has the MOST\nheadroom (31 places), which makes it the ONLY literal that overflows at the maximum shift a\n`< 32` loop can produce.  I filtered on \"little headroom = suspicious\" and thereby removed the\nsingle literal whose headroom exactly matches the commonest loop bound in the tree.\n\n> **AN ABSENCE RECORD IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE FILTER THAT PRODUCED IT, AND MY WARRANT NAMED THE\n> WRONG THING.**  I recorded the warrant as `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` -- a fact about the two sites the\n> filter DID surface -- when the load-bearing assumption was the FILTER ITSELF.  A warrant should\n> name what would falsify the record, and what falsified it was the exclusion, not the constant.\n\n**The `PAGEMASK_SHIFT` half stands**: those two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites really are safe, for the\nreason given.  What is retracted is the universal claim that nothing else in the tree has the shape.\n\n*Filed to [[shiftubcensus]], which is the row for this class and was already marked HONESTLY\nPARTIAL.  Recorded here too because THIS row is where the false absence was written.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*  #511, commit c7d7414 (NOT 7a41b3f).  `0x800 << dev' -> `0x800U << dev' -- VERIFIED LIVE AT HEAD.  The witness is NOT a new probe: it is the existing lca_confbus_probe run against the ASAN/UBSAN build; build/ stayed 3/4 with `bus kill' still red, which is the control that the differential measured the UB and not something else.  PASS 2 IS ON DISK (artifacts/lcashiftub/pass2_c7d7414/, six seats): FOUR SEATS REFUSED the `tag is already a wider unsigned' reason -- the TOKEN was ratified and the REASON corrected in the product comment, which is the advice-survives-its-reason shape.  Detector is a UBSAN differential and is EXPLICITLY NOT gate-wired (`ubsanshiftlane`); sibling `1 << 31` scanners in dev_uninorth.c and dev_v3.c are filed to `shiftubcensus`.  LOW is right: language UB, no measured wrong guest value, intended codegen on this compiler. CLOSED 2026-09-17 on the grok triage (regress/artifacts/<row>/ and _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_inwork.txt), which REFUTED THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISES: I asked about four rows with `commits` EMPTY and `sev: None`.  BOTH WERE MY QUERY BUGS -- I read `r.get(\"severity\")` when the field is `sev` (a wrong key and an absent value render IDENTICALLY), and I never queried `commits` at all, carrying `no commit yet` from a table that was true on 2026-09-07 and stale from 2026-09-08.  All four have SHAs; all four have severities.  The only real finding was that `state` was left `in work` after attribution was restored -- a lingering record, which is the resume-file rule applied to the ledger. NOT COMBINABLE.  The reopening rule was applied pairwise and every pair fails it: different files, different semantic classes, different detectors and oracles.  The shared `cpu_alpha_palcode.c, memory_alpha.c` file column was a CORRELATE -- the seat-audit derived it from the COMMIT's first src/ paths, and 7a41b3f is titled \"#505 #506 #507 #508\" and touches ~190 files.  `sgirergbroundtrip` is the control: same SHA, same bogus file column, an SGI RGB defect in dev_sgi_re.c, already closed as its own round.  More witnesses make more ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *\"I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet\"* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open \"on packet-only review\", which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file's last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *\"row 9's 'not applied' and 'unproven' descriptions are stale locally\"* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *\"an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.\"* *** THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *\"named and deliberately not applied\"*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5's LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *\"'#481's rows' is insufficient\"*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek's HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *\"edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.\"*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *\"combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does\"* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *\"these are coverage predictions, not newly run mutants.\"*  *** AN UNREAD DISSENT FROM 2026-09-07 THAT NAMED TODAY'S `wiringcliff` FINDING TEN DAYS EARLY, AND FROM THE PACKET ALONE.  Recorded 2026-09-17. ***  deepseek's **NOT RATIFY** on this row rests on its point 3: *\"Is shipping a fix whose only detector is an unwired differential acceptable?  **No.**  The packet states the differential is not gate-wired, so a regression would be caught by nothing.\"* **On 2026-09-17 the Opus 5 seat MEASURED exactly that** -- `lca_confbus_probe.py` is referenced by no gate, and for this row *\"the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes is the entire fix, reverted.\"*  *A packet-fed seat named the class; a measuring seat bought the instance ten days later; the answer was on disk the whole time.*  That is the carrier's own reading-seat-buys-the-class lesson, with the cost being the ten days. *** AND ITS POINT 5 IS NOW SETTLED -- AN EMPTY RESULT WITH ITS DENOMINATOR. ***  It asked *\"did I miss a sibling?  NOT CHECKABLE from packet ... need a grep for `0x800 <<` or signed left shift with variable\"* and named the settling command.  Run 2026-09-17 over `src/`: **15 candidate sites**; **13 already carry a `(uint32_t)` cast** (the PowerPC `cr` family); one is a COMMENT (`cpu_mips_coproc.c`); one is this row's OWN fix comment describing the old code.  **ONE live candidate: `dev_sgi_mec.c` `0x01 << dma_ptr_nr`** -- and it is SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION, because `if (dma_ptr_nr >= 4)` breaks TWO LINES ABOVE the shift, so the count is at most 3.  **ZERO siblings.**  *A seat named the settling command and nobody ran it for ten days; running it took one grep and closed the question.* *Its other points, recorded rather than actioned: the comment is too long for a one-token change (style, and the round disagreed deliberately); and it confirmed the arithmetic -- `0x800` = 2^11, `dev == 20` gives 2^31 > INT_MAX, `dev == 19` gives 2^30 which fits, loop bound `dev < 21`, so exactly one iteration overflows.*  *** L4 -- I RECORDED THE BRIEF'S OWN HEADINGS AS CODEX'S ANSWER ON ALL NINE ROWS.  Found by the Fable seat, corrected 2026-09-17. ***  My extraction matched the first line containing the row id and a word from `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` -- and **`CLOSE` matched the word `closed` inside the brief's own heading**, which codex echoes.  So nine cells read `### N. <id> (SEV, closed) -- <title>`: the packet, handed back as the seat's verdict. *** IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW AND FABLE SAID WHY: \"an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.\" ***  The blank that would have been caught was filled with something that looks like an answer. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* Codex's real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY 'SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS' ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET'S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  My own brief's S0 told the seats *\"an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction\"* -- which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, *discharged by naming the file* -- row 2's condition (c), row 8's figures, deepseek's rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *\"the file-reading seats verified it\" is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5's arithmetic, row 5's unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** glm's *\"nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire\"* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** **THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex's row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into \"the file-reading seats said CLOSE\".  *** THREE MORE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260907_221353), AND THEY CONVERGED ON ONE SETTLING FACT THAT NOBODY EVER LOOKED UP -- ONE GREP, ELEVEN DAYS UNREAD. ***  glm, kimi and minimax independently named THE SAME single missing fact as the only thing standing between the round and a clean pass: THE DECLARED TYPE OF `tag' in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf).  kimi put it plainest: 'What settles Q1 (the only open technical question): one line -- the declaration of `tag'.'  *** SETTLED 2026-09-18 BY READING IT: `int tag, type1, bus, dev, func, reg;' -- a plain int. ***  AND THE CONSEQUENCE, derived rather than asserted: in the fixed expression `tag & (0x800U << dev)' the UNSIGNED operand forces `tag' to be converted to unsigned int by the usual arithmetic conversions, so the AND is evaluated in unsigned and its TRUTH VALUE is identical to the pre-fix expression's.  The fix removes the signed-overflow UB (`0x800 << 20' on a plain int is 2^31, which does not fit) WITHOUT changing behaviour on any real target.  **THE ROUND'S UNIVERSAL NO-CHANGE CLAIM HOLDS**, and it is now settled by a reading rather than resting on an unverified premise -- which is what all three seats asked for.  OTHER POINTS THEY RAISED, none of them live defects: glm flagged P3 as 'the round's weakest settled premise -- asserted, uninlined, and precisely the shape seven seats ratified last time' (the premise-audit class, correctly identified); kimi noted a DUPLICATE CROSS-REFERENCE (the trailing /* #511 */ on the fixed line plus the citation inside the block -- keep one) and that the control's 3/4 invariant score should be quoted in the `ubsanshiftlane' row as evidence; minimax noted `0x800U << dev' is well-defined only if unsigned int is at least 32 bits, true on every host this project targets.  *** PROGRESS: 18 of 23 READ.  FIVE REMAIN (panel_20260827_134019, -> wdcaltctrlbit [closed]). ***",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40161:     "note": "*** AN UNREAD DISSENT FROM 2026-09-07 THAT NAMED TODAY'S `wiringcliff` FINDING TEN DAYS EARLY, AND FROM THE PACKET ALONE.  Recorded 2026-09-17. ***  deepseek's **NOT RATIFY** on this row rests on its point 3: *\"Is shipping a fix whose only detector is an unwired differential acceptable?  **No.**  The packet states the differential is not gate-wired, so a regression would be caught by nothing.\"* **On 2026-09-17 the Opus 5 seat MEASURED exactly that** -- `lca_confbus_probe.py` is referenced by no gate, and for this row *\"the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes is the entire fix, reverted.\"*  *A packet-fed seat named the class; a measuring seat bought the instance ten days later; the answer was on disk the whole time.*  That is the carrier's own reading-seat-buys-the-class lesson, with the cost being the ten days. *** AND ITS POINT 5 IS NOW SETTLED -- AN EMPTY RESULT WITH ITS DENOMINATOR. ***  It asked *\"did I miss a sibling?  NOT CHECKABLE from packet ... need a grep for `0x800 <<` or signed left shift with variable\"* and named the settling command.  Run 2026-09-17 over `src/`: **15 candidate sites**; **13 already carry a `(uint32_t)` cast** (the PowerPC `cr` family); one is a COMMENT (`cpu_mips_coproc.c`); one is this row's OWN fix comment describing the old code.  **ONE live candidate: `dev_sgi_mec.c` `0x01 << dma_ptr_nr`** -- and it is SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION, because `if (dma_ptr_nr >= 4)` breaks TWO LINES ABOVE the shift, so the count is at most 3.  **ZERO siblings.**  *A seat named the settling command and nobody ran it for ten days; running it took one grep and closed the question.* *Its other points, recorded rather than actioned: the comment is too long for a one-token change (style, and the round disagreed deliberately); and it confirmed the arithmetic -- `0x800` = 2^11, `dev == 20` gives 2^31 > INT_MAX, `dev == 19` gives 2^30 which fits, loop bound `dev < 21`, so exactly one iteration overflows.*",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40197:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) while scoping an unrelated device, and\nverified at both ends.\n\n    memory_alpha.c   (*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift;\n    memory_rw.c      offset = paddr & offset_mask;\n\nThe walk returns a PAGE-BASE address, so `paddr & offset_mask' is ZERO and every access within a page\nresolves to that page's first bytes.\n\n*** THE HEDGE IS THE SEAT'S OWN AND IT IS CORRECT: the DIRECT-MAPPED paths avoid it. ***  Both early returns\n-- K0SEG (`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffffULL') and the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap hack -- MASK the vaddr and therefore\nKEEP the offset bits.  Only the real walk drops them, and it is reached in user mode, or outside K0SEG once\nptbr != 0.\n\n**REACHABILITY IS THE OPEN QUESTION AND IT BOUNDS THE SEVERITY BOTH WAYS.**  memory_alpha.c carries seven\nTODOs and an explicit fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented\") +\nexit(1), so Alpha VM here is immature and a bootable configuration may never reach the walk -- in which case\nthis is latent rather than live.  **HIGH is PROVISIONAL on that**: if a guest reaches it the effect is total\nmemory-access corruption; if none can, the row is documented-only.  A round takes the reachability question\nFIRST; the fix itself is adding the offset back.\n\nThe function is live, not dead code: cpu_alpha.c assigns `cpu->translate_v2p = alpha_translate_v2p' and\ncpu_dyntrans.c calls it.\n\n*** REACHABILITY PASS, codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt).  THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED BY\nEXHAUSTIVE ENUMERATION -- AND MY OWN SUMMARY OF THE IMPACT WAS TOO BROAD.  Corrected here. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, and by walking the values rather than arguing:** *\"I enumerated all 8,192 offsets: the walk\nexpression produces offset zero throughout, differing from the offset-preserving address for 8,191 offsets.\nThe masking paths preserve offsets; exhaustive enumeration also confirmed preservation throughout all three\nbootstrap alias regions.\"*  So the defect is real, the direct-mapped arms are genuinely safe, and there are\nTHREE bootstrap alias regions rather than the two I had read.\n\n*** MY OVERCLAIM, CORRECTED: this row said `every walked access would land at its page base'.  THAT IS TOO\nBROAD. ***  codex: *\"Slow-path accesses consume the incorrect physical offset, but cached Alpha loads/stores\ncalculate `addr & 8191' themselves.  Subsequent cache hits can therefore access the correct within-page\nbytes.\"*  So the corruption is confined to the SLOW PATH; once a page is cached, the load/store instructions\nrecompute the offset and read the right bytes.  **That materially narrows the blast radius** and it is the\nsecond time tonight this seat has narrowed one of my summaries -- the first being the class-scan denominator.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- the answer is NOT `latent', and it is not `live' either.**  codex found *\"no source barrier\nmaking a guest-instruction witness impossible on the existing description\"*, and named the smallest decisive\nobservation: one COMPLETED guest data access on the existing `eb164' machine that reaches the walk with a\nnonzero page offset and whose caller completes the access on the returned page-base address.  **A PTBR\nwatchpoint or a walker-entry breakpoint is explicitly NOT enough** -- reaching the walk is not the same as an\naccess completing wrongly.\n\n**What it could not settle, and said so plainly:** *\"I cannot name a demonstrated working Alpha OS boot from\nthe allowed files.\"*  So the honest status is **WITNESSABLE IN PRINCIPLE, UNWITNESSED IN FACT** -- which is\na different thing from latent, and it is what a round takes first.\n\n**HIGH stands but its warrant changes**: not `every access is corrupted' (false), but `the slow path is\ncorrupted for 8,191 of 8,192 offsets, on a path with no established guest that reaches it'.\n\n*** THE BOOT QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND IT REVERSES MY CONCERN: ALL FOUR ALPHA ROWS ARE COLD-DEBUGGER\nREACHABLE.  NO OS BOOT IS REQUIRED.  codex, 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt). ***\n\nI had flagged the risk that the Alpha queue was accumulating well-analysed rows on a machine nothing can\nexercise.  It is not: *\"EB164 or AlphaServer 4100 provides the required unmodified machine description.\nThere is no architectural requirement to boot an OS first.\"*  Debugger word writes go through the memory\nhelpers, Alpha fetches and decodes through its actual memory path, and the shared breakpoint machinery\napplies -- which is exactly the rung-3 cold-debugger shape this project's ladder admits.\n\nIts classification of all four:\n\n    page-walk drops the offset      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   needs page-table state; the bootstrap\n                                                              identity mapping bypasses the walk\n    PALcode register mismatch       COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   swpctx is reached by guest dispatch\n    unsupported fault-delivery      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   failed access returns without an exception\n    guest-reachable process exit    COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   the bootstrap translator's special branch\n\n**The qualification on THIS row is the one that matters for its witness:** the walk needs page-table state,\nand *the ordinary bootstrap identity mapping bypasses the walk*.  So a probe must first establish a non-zero\n`apcb_ptbr' and a valid three-level table -- which is real work, and is why this row is still the hardest of\nthe five despite being the most severe if reached.\n\n**[[alphaswpctxptbr]] is a confound for that witness and must be fixed first:** it clobbers `apcb_ptbr` with\nthe flags word, so any probe that sets up a context via `swpctx` and then observes a bad translation cannot\nsay which defect produced it.\n\n*** THE CONFOUND IS NOW MEASURED, NOT SUSPECTED.  2026-09-07. ***\n[[alphaswpctxptbr]] has a rung-3 witness as of today: on the committed build, `PCB_A+16` reads the\nFLAGS word (`0xbbbb2222`) where the ptbr (`0xaaaa1111`) is required -- `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`,\nHEAD 7/9, artifacts under `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/`.\n\nSo the ordering this row already asserted is now backed by a measurement: **a witness for the\npage-walk offset defect must either fix the swap first or establish `apcb_ptbr` by a route that does\nnot go through `swpctx`** -- otherwise a bad translation cannot be attributed to the walk.  The\nharness's own bootstrap shows such a route exists (debugger `put d` writes the PCB directly while\n`ptbr == 0`), which is the more promising of the two.\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/grok_witness_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: BUILDABLE on the committed tree, and it **does not wait on [[alphaswpctxptbr]]** -- which\nrefutes the premise this brief was written around. ***\n\n**S4 WAS TOO STRONG.**  `swpctx` IS the only writer of `apcb_ptbr` (verified: the debugger exports\nonly `pc` and the GPRs; `wrvptptr` writes the unused `vptptr`; `machine_alpha.c` puts the L1 PFN in\n**`a1`**, not the PCB).  **But the swap is only a confound if `ptbr` and `flags` DIFFER.**\n\n> **PUT THE SAME L1 PFN IN BOTH PCB FIELDS.**  Then the defective line and the corrected line both\n> leave `apcb_ptbr = L1_PFN`, and the walk is armed identically either way.  One `call_pal 0x30`,\n> and the confound is gone without fixing anything.\n\n*And the inverse is a trap: putting the PFN only in `flags` with `ptbr = 0` arms the walk on HEAD and\nleaves IDENTITY MAPPING on the fixed PAL -- so post-fix green would be the bootstrap, not the repair.*\n\n**S3 IS SHARPER THAN I WROTE, AND IT KILLS MY Q3.**  The fast path caches the page from\n`paddr & ~offset_mask`, so the cached host page is the **correct physical page** and a later hit at\nANY in-page offset reads the right bytes.  **The walked paddr is wrong only for the FILL.**  So *what\nN avoids the cached path* is the wrong question -- **N does not select the slow path.**  The design is\n`N = 8`, cold cache, **ONE shot, and do not retry the load: the second access is a hit and goes green\non HEAD.**  (An unaligned N does force the generic path, but that is an emulator gap rather than\nAlpha, and a later alignment check would kill the witness for the wrong reason.)\n\n*** THE PLANTED-PAST-RAM EQUIVALENT FOR THIS PROBE IS NOT AN ADDRESS BOUND -- IT IS PFN-vs-BYTE. ***\n`apcb_ptbr = 0x01050000` gives `base = 0x01050000 << 13`, past RAM, `memory_paddr_to_hostaddr` NULL,\n`not_found`.  **The PCB and the PTE high halves hold PFNs, not byte addresses.**\n\n**THE TABLE, concrete and off both the PROM pocket (`0x10000`/`0x10100`/`0x12000`/`0x14000`) and the\nsibling probes' scratch:**\n\n    L1   0x01050000  PFN 0x828      L1[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x829) | VALID\n    L2   0x01052000  PFN 0x829      L2[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x82A) | VALID\n    L3   0x01054000  PFN 0x82A      L3[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x82B) | VALID\n    data 0x01056000  PFN 0x82B      apcb_ptbr = 0x828   <- the PFN\n\nTest vaddr in `[0, 8192)` so `i1 = i2 = i3 = 0`.  **Only `ALPHA_PTE_VALID` is load-bearing** -- the\nwalk never tests KR/KW/UR/UW, FAULT_ON_*, ASM or granularity.  *\"Do not set bits the walk does not\nimplement and then call them required\"* -- the addition licence, applied to a probe.\n\n**THE THREE-WAY ORACLE, which is better than the two-way I proposed, and rests on a fact I had\nwrong.**  My Q4 said a failed walk \"faults or returns zero\".  **False for the guest-visible\nregister:** `LS_GENERIC_N` returns on `memory_rw` failure **without writing the destination**.  So\npre-set the destination to a third sentinel `S`:\n\n    S unchanged  the walk never completed\n    0            the walk succeeded onto UNMAPPED physical (memblock NULL still returns ok)\n    A            the walk hit the right page at offset 0   <- THE DEFECT\n    B            correct\n\n**THE POLARITY TRAP, and it is the `lcaconfwidth` inversion wearing different clothes:** plant `B`\nTHROUGH the walk (a `put d` or `stq` at `vaddr+N` once `ptbr` is live) and on HEAD that store lands\nat `data+0`; the later load of `vaddr+N` also lands at `data+0` and reads `B`.  **HEAD goes green,\nmeasuring the defect against itself.**  A cousin: planting at vaddr `0`/`N` while `ptbr == 0` hits\nPHYSICAL `0`/`N`, not the data page, and both polarities collapse.  **Plant `A` and `B` only via\nidentity-physical or K0SEG of the DATA PAGE, before the walk is used.**\n\n**CONTROLS:** `data0 k0` and `dataN k0` (K0SEG reads of the same physical page -- they do not fill the\ntest vaddr's translation slot, different key), `walk mapped` (dest not 0 and not S), and the ABSENCE\nof `alpha_translate_v2p: ... wasn't found`.  *`A` on the walked load with those green is itself the\nproof the walk ran -- a stronger channel than a same-decode round-trip, because the defect signature\nnames the page.*\n\n**READ beats WRITE as the witness** (one `ldq`, three-way register, single `want`).  A walked `stq`\nis the same mechanism and needs the same three table pages -- *the write form does not save a PTE* --\nand its read-back must go through K0SEG, since a walked read-back is a cache hit that hides HEAD.\n\n**Four smaller facts, each load-bearing:** code must stay at `k0(P_CODE)` after `ptbr` is live (a\nphysical-looking PC walks with `i2=32` and a 3-PTE table does not map it); stay in kernel mode, since\nuser mode makes K0SEG I-fetch walk; `pte2`/`pte3` invalid additionally `fatal(\"TODO: ...\")`, useful as\na setup channel; and `store_64bit_word` sign-extends addresses whose bits 63:32 are clear, so physical\n`put d` stays below 2 GB or uses K0SEG.\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 5/6 -- THE OFFSET DROP IS NOW\nMEASURED, not derived. ***\n\n    data0 k0     0xaaaa0000aaaa   0xaaaa0000aaaa   ok    CONTROL: A really is at data+0\n    dataN k0     0xbbbb0000bbbb   0xbbbb0000bbbb   ok    CONTROL: B really is at data+N\n    walk found   absent           absent           ok    CONTROL: walked to a valid L3\n    walk stored  0xaaaa0000aaaa   NOT 0x55000055   ok    CONTROL: the load DID write the register\n    walk mapped  0xaaaa0000aaaa   NOT 0x0          ok    CONTROL: not unmapped physical\n    walk off     0xaaaa0000aaaa   0xbbbb0000bbbb   FAIL  the walked load returned data+0\n\n**A real guest `ldq` of `vaddr+8`, through a hand-built three-level page table, on an unmodified\nmachine description, returned the value at the page BASE.**  The three-way sentinel earned its place:\nthe register was written (so the walk completed), the result was not 0 (so it did not land on\nunmapped physical), and it was `A` (so the walk found the right PAGE and dropped the OFFSET).  Those\nthree are separable only because the sentinel exists -- a two-way oracle would have reported the same\nred for all three causes.\n\n**EVERY ONE OF GROK'S DESIGN CORRECTIONS WAS LOAD-BEARING AND ALL OF THEM HELD IN PRACTICE:**\nthe same-PFN-in-both-PCB-fields trick defeated the [[alphaswpctxptbr]] confound without fixing it,\nso this row did NOT wait on that repair; the PFN-not-byte-address point is what keeps `base` inside\nRAM; K0SEG planting kept the setup off the walk (planting `B` through the walk would have made HEAD\ngreen against itself); and the single cold shot mattered -- a retry is a cache hit that reads the\ncorrect bytes and greens on HEAD.\n\n*Two defects of my own, caught before the run: dead placeholder code, and a 40-bit sentinel handed to\na `load_const` that builds 32-bit constants -- it would have truncated silently and the sentinel row\nwould have compared the wrong value.  The row now pins a value `load_const` can actually build.*\n\n*** GROK FIX DESIGN 2026-09-07.  VERDICT: RATIFY THE ASSIGNMENT, NOT THE ROUND -- the same\nruling as `#507`, and it REFUTED MY OWN STATED WORRY while finding a worse one. ***\n\n**THE FIX:**\n\n    (*return_paddr) = (ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift)\n        | (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1));\n\n`|` and `+` are numerically identical here (the shifted PFN has bits 12:0 clear), but they are\ndifferent SENTENCES: **`|` is every other walk in this tree** -- ARM, m88k, MIPS, PPC, SH -- while\n`+` is this function's fixed bootstrap windows.  **And derive the mask from `pageSizeShift`, not a\nliteral**: `:88`'s `0x1fff` is correct for a fixed 8 KB PROM pocket, and hardcoding it beside\n`<< pageSizeShift` is how a mask and its shift drift apart.\n\n*The parentheses are load-bearing: `1 << pageSizeShift - 1` is `0x1000`, not `0x1fff`.  **The\nexisting `N = 8` row DOES kill that one** (`8 & 0x1000 == 0` -> VAL_A).*\n\n*** MY `0xfff`/`0x3fff` PAIRING WAS FALSE, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS. ***\nI wrote that a mask of `0xfff` or `0x3fff` would be *\"correct at N = 8 and wrong further into the\npage\"*.  **`0x3fff` is not wrong anywhere in the page:** the extra bit is bit 13, and bit 13 of an\noffset is zero for every byte of an 8 KB page.  `+0x1008` still passes under it.  **`0xfff` is the\ndangerous one** -- it is the 4 KB copy (`MRW_OFFSET_MASK` on every non-Alpha build,\n`DYNTRANS_PAGESIZE` 4096), and it leaves **the upper 4 KB of every walked page still broken**.\n\n**SIX LESSER EDITS ALSO SCORE 6/6 on the committed probe** (`VADDR = 0`, `N = 8`): masks `0xfff`,\n`0x3fff`, `0x7fff` (a copy of `:81` rather than `:88`), any mask including bit 3, **unmasked\n`+ vaddr`** -- correct only because the VPN is 0 at page 0 -- and a hardcoded `| 8`.  **A second cold\nrow is owed**, at a vaddr whose VPN is non-zero and an offset in the upper 4 KB.\n\n**THREE CALLERS ENUMERATED, and one is a THIRD consequence I did not know:**\n\n    memory_rw.c MEMORY_RW_ONE_PAGE   needs the FULL address     <- the defect\n    cpu_dyntrans.c I-fetch miss      tolerates either; strips the offset itself\n    debugger_cmds.c watchpoint       needs the FULL address     <- A WALKED WATCHPOINT TODAY\n                                                                   LANDS AT THE PAGE BASE\n\n**No caller does `paddr + (vaddr & mask)`, so the mirror defect is absent and a post-fix 6/6 is not\nhiding a double-add.**  Q5 confirmed independently: the fill strips before caching\n(`memory_paddr_to_hostaddr(mem, paddr & ~offset_mask, ...)`), `update_translation_table` asserts\npage alignment, and the fast path uses the VIRTUAL offset -- so the walked offset is consumed exactly\nonce.\n\n**KEEP THE `// TODO.`**  Restoring the offset leaves it true: `flags` is never read\n(`FLAG_WRITEFLAG`, `FLAG_INSTR`, `FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` all ignored), `ALPHA_PTE_KR/KW/UR/UW` and\n`FAULT_ON_*` are untested, granularity hints are ignored, and **`return 2` means WRITABLE -- so\n`memory_rw` caches every walked Alpha page as writable.**  *Deleting the comment would record the\ntranslator as finished.  It is not.*\n\n**THREE MORE, FILED NOT FIXED:** `:81` is the WRONG neighbour to copy (`0x7fff`, a 32 KB mask -- a\nseat told to *\"copy the in-function idiom\"* can copy the wrong one, and the current witness cannot\nsee it); `flags` being dead means a failed walk still prints `wasn't found` even under\n`FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS`, which is exactly the debugger-watchpoint path; and **the function uses TWO NAMES\nfor 13** -- `ALPHA_PAGESHIFT` on the L1/L2 physical addresses, local `pageSizeShift` on the indices\nand the final shift -- so the new mask must couple to `pageSizeShift` or the two can diverge on one\nline.\n\n*** `#508` MEASURED: 5/7 -> 7/7, SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    walk off   0xaaaa0000aaaa -> 0xbbbb0000bbbb\n    walk vpn   0xcccc0000cccc -> 0xdddd0000dddd     <- the row the fix review demanded\n\nFive controls green on both runs.  All four trees propagated; three rebuilt with the two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH; `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold stands.\n\n*The `walk vpn` row is the third instance today of a fix review ratifying an edit and refusing the\nROUND because the witness could not distinguish the fix from a lesser one.  In all three cases the\nmissing row cost one readback and caught something the author had not considered.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/memory_alpha.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/cpus/memory_alpha.c\n                                   contains the `#508' round tag 4 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*  #508.  The page-table walk returned `PFN << pageSizeShift' (the PAGE BASE) and dropped the in-page offset; HEAD now ORs `vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1)' back in -- VERIFIED LIVE AT HEAD.  Two-direction witness with a second row (`walk vpn') that exists because the fix review REFUSED a 6/6 which six lesser masks also scored.  NO KILL TABLE: the detector IS the witness (check_probe_wiring classifies it CLASS_WITNESS) and NO GATE RUNS IT -- that is `wiringcliff` debt on its own row, the same footing as the other closed Alpha witnesses, not a hold on this one. CLOSED 2026-09-17 on the grok triage (regress/artifacts/<row>/ and _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_inwork.txt), which REFUTED THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISES: I asked about four rows with `commits` EMPTY and `sev: None`.  BOTH WERE MY QUERY BUGS -- I read `r.get(\"severity\")` when the field is `sev` (a wrong key and an absent value render IDENTICALLY), and I never queried `commits` at all, carrying `no commit yet` from a table that was true on 2026-09-07 and stale from 2026-09-08.  All four have SHAs; all four have severities.  The only real finding was that `state` was left `in work` after attribution was restored -- a lingering record, which is the resume-file rule applied to the ledger. NOT COMBINABLE.  The reopening rule was applied pairwise and every pair fails it: different files, different semantic classes, different detectors and oracles.  The shared `cpu_alpha_palcode.c, memory_alpha.c` file column was a CORRELATE -- the seat-audit derived it from the COMMIT's first src/ paths, and 7a41b3f is titled \"#505 #506 #507 #508\" and touches ~190 files.  `sgirergbroundtrip` is the control: same SHA, same bogus file column, an SGI RGB defect in dev_sgi_re.c, already closed as its own round.  More witnesses make more ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *\"I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet\"* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open \"on packet-only review\", which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file's last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *\"row 9's 'not applied' and 'unproven' descriptions are stale locally\"* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *\"an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.\"* *** THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *\"named and deliberately not applied\"*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5's LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *\"'#481's rows' is insufficient\"*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek's HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *\"edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.\"*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *\"combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does\"* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *\"these are coverage predictions, not newly run mutants.\"*  *** L4 -- I RECORDED THE BRIEF'S OWN HEADINGS AS CODEX'S ANSWER ON ALL NINE ROWS.  Found by the Fable seat, corrected 2026-09-17. ***  My extraction matched the first line containing the row id and a word from `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` -- and **`CLOSE` matched the word `closed` inside the brief's own heading**, which codex echoes.  So nine cells read `### N. <id> (SEV, closed) -- <title>`: the packet, handed back as the seat's verdict. *** IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW AND FABLE SAID WHY: \"an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.\" ***  The blank that would have been caught was filled with something that looks like an answer. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* Codex's real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY 'SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS' ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET'S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  My own brief's S0 told the seats *\"an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction\"* -- which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, *discharged by naming the file* -- row 2's condition (c), row 8's figures, deepseek's rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *\"the file-reading seats verified it\" is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5's arithmetic, row 5's unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** glm's *\"nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire\"* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** **THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex's row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into \"the file-reading seats said CLOSE\".",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40617:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **RAISED BY THE CODEX SEAT** under the found-something-else licence, on a brief that asked\nonly whether Alpha machines can run anything (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt) -- and VERIFIED by reading before\nfiling.\n\n`PAL_OSF1_swpctx' loads a new context and assigns it into the pcb.  **`apcb_ptbr' is assigned TWICE and\n`apcb_flags' is never assigned at all:**\n\n    // Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously:\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ksp    = new_ksp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_usp    = new_usp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_ptbr;      <- correct\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_cpc    = new_cpc;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_asn    = new_asn;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_unique = new_unique;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_flags;     <- *** should be apcb_flags ***\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv0 = new_decrsv0;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv1 = new_decrsv1;\n\n*** WHY THIS OUTRANKS THE OTHER ALPHA ROWS: `apcb_ptbr' IS THE PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER. ***\n`alpha_translate_v2p()' opens with `uint64_t base = cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr << ALPHA_PAGESHIFT;' -- so\nafter ANY `swpctx' the page-table walk reads its tables from an address derived from the FLAGS word.  Every\ntranslation after a context switch walks garbage.\n\n**And `apcb_flags' keeps the OUTGOING context's value**, because nothing ever writes it -- a second, quieter\ndefect from the same line.\n\n**THE COMMENT DIRECTLY ABOVE ASSERTS WHAT THE CODE DOES NOT DO:** *\"Update all variables in the pcb\nsimultaneously\"*.  Two of the nine are wrong -- one clobbered, one never set.  That is the second time in\nthis one function that a comment states an intent the code contradicts; the other is [[alphaswpctxreg]].\n\n**FIX: one token** -- the second `apcb_ptbr' becomes `apcb_flags'.  As with its sibling, the intent is not in\ndoubt: `new_flags' is loaded from the context block and would otherwise be dead, and `apcb_flags' exists in\nthe struct and would otherwise never be written.\n\n**REACHABILITY: cold-debugger reachable, per the same pass** -- `swpctx' is an active handler reached by\nordinary guest instruction dispatch with no OS dependency.  A witness plants a `call_pal 0x30' and observes\n`apcb_ptbr' afterwards.\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH [[alphapfnoffset]] IS WORTH STATING, because it changes how that round should be\ndesigned: this defect CORRUPTS THE WALK'S BASE, and that one CORRUPTS THE WALK'S RESULT. ***  A witness for\neither must control for the other -- a probe that does a `swpctx' and then observes a bad translation cannot\nattribute it without pinning which of the two produced it.  **Fix this one first**: it is certain, one token,\nand it removes a confound from the harder row.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).  THE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED AND TWO OF MY\nSUPPORTING PREMISES WERE WRONG.  Corrected here. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"A and B are defects, and both proposed corrections are right.  They are TWO changes under your\nreopening rule.  `apcb_flags is never written' is false; no third destination typo was found.\"*\n\n**CORRECTION 1 -- `apcb_flags' IS WRITTEN, and I filed the opposite as fact.**  This row said it *\"is never\nassigned at all\"*.  `grep -rn apcb_flags src/' returns `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:221' -- **`wrfen' assigns it**\nfrom `r[ALPHA_A0]' and then writes it back to PCB memory, and `swpctx' itself READS the field when saving the\noutgoing context.\n\n**The honest statement is narrower and still a defect:** `swpctx' never assigns `apcb_flags' WHEN LOADING A\nNEW CONTEXT, so the incoming context's flags are silently dropped and the outgoing context's value persists\nacross the switch.  That is a real defect; *never written anywhere* was not.\n\n**CORRECTION 2 -- `new_flags' is NOT dead, and the distinction matters.**  I wrote that it *\"would otherwise\nbe dead\"*.  codex: it *\"has exactly one use after its declaration: the erroneous PTBR assignment.  It is\notherwise unused, but **not dead in the current program**: it changes PTBR.\"*  My phrasing implied the\ncompiler would flag it; it would not, because the erroneous line consumes it.\n\n**A QUALIFICATION ON THE SIBLING [[alphaswpctxreg]] THAT BOUNDS ITS IMPACT:** the handler captures its input\ninto a LOCAL `a0' before dispatch, so overwriting `r[ALPHA_A0]' does **not** change which context is loaded.\nThat defect is therefore purely a wrong RETURN VALUE -- it does not corrupt the switch itself.  I had not\nestablished that, and it is the difference between a reporting bug and a state-corruption bug.\n\n**SCOPE: TWO CHANGES, NOT ONE.**  I intended to fix both in one diff -- same function, same class.  codex\nrules they are two under the reopening rule.  **Accepted**: the observables differ (a guest register versus\ninternal PCB state reachable only through a translation), so one test cannot cover both without a new\nobservation, which is exactly what that rule forbids.\n\n**MY Q3 HYPOTHESIS WAS REFUTED, and recording that matters as much as a finding.**  I said a third\nwrong-destination assignment was *\"more likely than not\"* given two in one function, and asked for the whole\nhandler to be read.  It was, and there is no third.  **A predicted class that does not materialise is a\nresult** -- and it stops the next round hunting for one.\n\n*** READ-ONLY AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_read_20260907.txt`).\nTHE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED FIELD BY FIELD, THE FIX IS ONE TOKEN, AND A PREMISE OF MINE ABOUT\nREACHABILITY WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN THE WITNESS. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"`swpctx` assigns `apcb_ptbr` twice, leaves `apcb_flags` unchanged, and uses all nine\nlocals.  Its return line writes `a0`.  Opcode-0 dispatch has no privilege check, but\n`alpha_palcode()` itself checks user mode before the switch.\"*\n\n**CONFIRMED, from the seat's own nine-row assignment table rather than from my description:**\n`apcb_ptbr` is assigned twice, `apcb_flags` never, and **all nine locals are used** -- so the defect\nis precisely a mis-typed destination, not a dropped load.  **The fix is ONE IDENTIFIER TOKEN on ONE\nLINE** (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`), which the seat states explicitly.  The `a0`-for-`v0` return is\nconfirmed as a second, separate defect in the same handler.\n\n*** THE CORRECTION, AND IT IS A HARNESS-BREAKER RATHER THAN A RECORDS NICETY. ***  I wrote -- in the\nbrief AND in the first draft of the Alpha harness -- that `call_pal` dispatch is unconditional.\n**Dispatch INTO `alpha_palcode()` is; the function then GATES ITS OWN SWITCH** at\n`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:183`:\n\n    uint64_t userMode = cpu->cd.alpha.ps & ALPHA_PSL_USERMODE;      /* 0x0008 */\n    if (userMode && !(palcode == 0x80 || 0x81 || 0x83 || 0xae ||\n                      0xaa || 0x86 || 0x9e || 0x92 || 0x9f)) {\n            fatal(\"[ Privileged Alpha PALcode called from user mode: TODO ]\");\n            cpu->running = 0;\n            return;\n    }\n\n**`0x30` is not on that whitelist**, so in user mode `call_pal 0x30` does not reach swpctx -- it\nHALTS THE CPU.  **That failure presents as a probe whose step never completes**, i.e. as a broken\nharness rather than as a mode problem, which is the worst kind of failure to debug.  Verified by\nreading the lines rather than taken on the seat's word.\n\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ps` has NO explicit initialiser anywhere in `src/`, so it is zero from the zeroed cpu\nstruct and USERMODE is clear -- kernel mode, and the gate does not fire.  **That is a derivation, and\nthe harness turns it into a measurement**: `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` now carries a `palgate`\nrow asserting the diagnostic is absent, and a `palalive` row asserting the machine survives a swpctx\ncall at all.\n\n*Note the shape: the premise that was wrong is the one I did NOT think to question -- I checked the\ninstruction decoder carefully and never looked inside the function it dispatches to.  That is the\nthird instance this week and it is the subject of the queued rule question\n[[sgilinearnegidxpostmortem]], which asks whether the premise-audit instruction needs a\nself-directed twin.  **This is its third data point and the first where a seat caught it before it\ncost anything.***\n\n**AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THIS WAS THE SECOND ATTEMPT.**  The first -- carrying a four-step witness\nDESIGN -- was REFUSED by the same seat as chain-shaped content (see [[codexbriefscoping]]).  The\nchain-free re-ask, which requests a field-by-field audit and names no procedure, was answered in\nfull.  **The design question went to grok instead**, so neither question was dropped.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN WAS REFUTED, BY THE SEAT THE REFUSED BRIEF WAS ROUTED TO.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_witness_20260907.txt`, 13.5 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"YES, a rung-3 witness is buildable on the three constructing Alpha machines -- but the\nproposed 'no MMU at all, two `swpctx`, read `A+16`' design is unsound as written if `A.flags` is\nnonzero.  After the first switch, `apcb_ptbr` IS the flags word, the identity map dies, and the\nsecond `call_pal` (and the save that is supposed to publish the bug) can fail to land or can\n`exit(1)` the host.  Use K0SEG for code and both PCBs, or keep `flags==0` and accept a weaker\noracle.  The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous LHS.\"*\n\n*** THE DESIGN WAS SELF-DEFEATING AND I HAD NOT SEEN IT: THE DEFECT DESTROYS THE PATH THE WITNESS\nNEEDS TO OBSERVE IT. ***  Verified in `memory_alpha.c` rather than taken on the seat's word, and the\nsource is sharper than the verdict:\n\n  * **K0SEG is checked BEFORE `base` is used at all** (`:62`): `if (!userMode && vaddr >=\n    ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE && vaddr <= ALPHA_K0SEG_END) { *return_paddr = vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff; return\n    2; }`.  A K0SEG access never consults `apcb_ptbr`, so grok's remedy is sound.\n    `ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE = 0xfffffc0000000000`; `ALPHA_PHYS_TO_K0SEG(x) = x | BASE`.\n  * **The identity map is an explicit 'UGLY hack' conditioned on `base == 0`** (`:73`).  That is the\n    ONLY reason a naive harness works at all -- `ptbr` is zero at reset.  **The moment the buggy\n    assignment makes `apcb_ptbr` nonzero, `base != 0` and every later access falls into a real\n    page-table walk over garbage**, with an `exit(1)` sitting on one vaddr at `:75-78`.\n  * **AND THE POLARITY TRAP grok did not state, which kills the 'keep flags==0' fallback too:** if\n    the marker is put in `A.ptbr` instead, then under the *FIXED* build `apcb_ptbr` becomes that\n    nonzero marker and the identity map dies on the FIX rather than on the defect.  **Neither\n    polarity survives with a nonzero value anywhere in that field.**  So K0SEG is not the better\n    option, it is the only one.\n\n**THE CORRECTED DESIGN, and it needs no 64-bit immediates:** `lda`/`ldah` build 32-bit constants\nonly, and a K0SEG address needs bit 63..42 set.  The way through is to **load the pointers BEFORE the\nfirst `swpctx`, while the identity map is still alive**: place the 64-bit K0SEG pointers in\nidentity-mapped scratch with the debugger's `put q`, have the guest `ldq` them into registers, and\nonly then issue the calls.  Every access after that carries a K0SEG address in a register and is\nimmune to whatever `apcb_ptbr` becomes.\n\n**A SEPARATE TRAP FOUND WHILE CHECKING THIS, which the harness avoids only by luck:** `memory_alpha.c:87`\nsilently remaps `(vaddr & ~0x1fff) == 0x20000000` to `PROM_ARGSPACE_PADDR` (0x12000).  The harness's\ncode at `0x20010000` and scratch at `0x20020000` fall OUTSIDE that 8 kB window -- but nothing said\nso, and a probe placed 64 kB lower would have had its stores quietly land in PROM argument space.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED:** *\"The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous\nLHS.\"*  Both seats agree the correction is `apcb_flags` at `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`.\n\n*** THE ROUTING WORKED AND IS THE REUSABLE LESSON. ***  codex REFUSED this brief as chain-shaped;\ngrok answered it in full, read the handler, the dispatch path and the existing harness, and refuted\nthe design on a mechanism I had missed.  **The chain-free re-ask to codex and the chain-shaped\noriginal to grok together cost one extra invocation and lost nothing** -- which is the argument the\n2026-09-09 adjudication is asked to rule on under [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** CODE REVIEW OF THE HARNESS, BEFORE ITS FIRST RUN.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_harness_20260907.txt` -- codex REFUSED this brief too (sixth\nrefusal, chain/witness shape), so it is grok's third answer to a brief codex would not take. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DO NOT TRUST THE FIRST RUN.  The lda/ldah/ldq bootstrap and the swpctx SAVE/RESTORE\nwalk are right.  The encodings that this file actually emits match the decoder.  `put d` is the right\ndebugger command.  What is wrong is the ADDRESS PLAN, and it makes three rows able to go green while\nmeasuring 'unmapped reads return 0', not the handler.\"*\n\n*** FINDING 1 -- FATAL, AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT AND GREEN. ***  The machine is built `-M 64`\n= 64 MB and every address in the file sat at `0x200xxxxx` (512 MB+).  K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff`, so **the K0SEG idea survives contact with `alpha_translate_v2p` and the\nPHYSICAL BACKING does not.**  And the failure mode is silent:\n`memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` only halts when `halt_on_nonexistant_memaccess` is set, i.e.\nunder `-T`.  **Without it, writes succeed and are DISCARDED and reads RETURN ZEROS** -- and an\ninstruction fetch of zeros is a real instruction, `call_pal 0`.\n\n**MY ORACLE MADE THAT UNDETECTABLE.**  It asserted `PCB_A+16 == 0` for the correct case, and 0 is\nalso what an unmapped read returns.  **The witness would have gone green having written nothing,\nread nothing, and executed `call_pal 0` off a zero-filled code page.**\n\n**REPAIRED, and the repair is better than moving the addresses:** K0SEG means translation never\nconsults `apcb_ptbr`, so `apcb_ptbr` may now hold a distinctive NONZERO value -- which the FIRST\ndesign could not allow, because a nonzero ptbr killed the identity map.  So:\n\n    correct handler  -> PCB_A+16 == PTBR_VAL  (0xaaaa1111)\n    the defect       -> PCB_A+16 == FLAGS_VAL (0xbbbb2222)\n    anything broken  -> 0, which is NEITHER, so BOTH rows red\n\nplus a third row `swpctx mapped` asserting the value is not 0 -- **because without it a probe whose\naddresses drifted back outside RAM would fail one row and pass the other, which reads exactly like a\nreal defect.**  Addresses moved inside 64 MB with an `assert` that says so.\n\n*** FINDING 2 -- `palalive` IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, AND I WROTE IT WHILE APPLYING THE RULE\nAGAINST THAT. ***  *\"`alive` is 'the pty is still readable'.  `alpha_palcode`'s default arm does\n`cpu->running = 0` and stays in the debugger because `-V` is single-step.  The emulator does not\nexit.  `palalive` stays green.\"*  **Kept as a LABELLED NON-ORACLE rather than deleted**, so the next\nreader sees why it is there and does not re-add it as a real row; the mode question is answered by\n`palgate`, which greps the diagnostic text.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- the encoder self-test does not test the decoder.**  It re-runs `load_const`'s own\n32-bit wrap masked with `& 0xffffffff`, while LDAH sign-extends through an `(int32_t)` cast --\n`0xdeadbeef` would be `0xffffffffdeadbeef` in a register and the self-test would still print ok.\n**It is sound only because every value this file uses has bit 31 clear.**  Correct by luck; recorded\nin the file as such rather than quietly fixed.\n\n**FINDING 4 -- `put q` is not merely 128-bit, it is UNIMPLEMENTED** (`\"quad-words: TODO\"`).  The\npremise was true and understated.\n\n**CONFIRMED, so the design work was not wasted:** encodings match the decoder; the bootstrap method\nis valid and nothing between the `put d` writes and the first `call_pal` touches `apcb_ptbr`; K0SEG\nis checked before `base`; PCB offsets match `struct alpha_pcb`; `ps` is zero from `zeroed_alloc`;\n`0x30` is not on the unprivileged whitelist.  It also supplied two facts I did not have:\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ctx` starts at `0x10100`, and PROM setup (on by default) sets `a0 = 2048`.\n\n*** THE REVIEW COST ONE INVOCATION AND SAVED A GREEN WITNESS THAT PROVED NOTHING. ***  The lifecycle\nsays ask a seat to try a rung-3 probe; this says ask a seat to READ the probe before running it, and\non this evidence the second is worth as much as the first.\n\n*** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND RUN, 2026-09-07.  THE DEFECT REPRODUCES ON THE COMMITTED BUILD. ***\n\n    row              got          want              \n    encoder          ok           ok                load_const sign correction, 9 vectors, offline\n    live             0x5a5a5a5a   0x5a5a5a5a        LIVENESS: guest store then load through RAM\n    exec             0x1234       0x1234            a guest LDAH/LDA pair reached a register\n    palgate          not gated    not gated         call_pal 0x30 is privileged; we are in kernel mode\n    palalive         alive        alive             NON-ORACLE, labelled as such\n    swpctx alive     alive        alive             the guest survives two swpctx calls in K0SEG\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111        FAIL -- the defect\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222    FAIL -- the defect's exact signature\n    swpctx mapped    0xbbbb2222   NOT 0x0           ok  -- the memory WAS there\n    ALPHAHARNESS_RESULT=7/9\n\n**THE MECHANISM, confirmed by the measurement rather than only by reading:** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`\ncase `0x30` LOADs the new context, sets `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr`, and then **overwrites it with\n`new_flags` a few lines later**.  The probe stages PCB_A with ptbr `0xaaaa1111` and flags\n`0xbbbb2222`, swaps A in, swaps B in (whose SAVE writes A's context back to `PCB_A+16`), and reads\nthat cell.  It holds the FLAGS.\n\n*** `swpctx mapped` IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PHANTOM, and it exists\nbecause a code review demanded it. ***  The grok harness review found that **every address in the\nfirst draft was planted past the end of RAM** -- `-M 64` gives 64 MB and the structures sat at\n`0x20010000`+ (512 MB) -- where writes are discarded and reads return zeros with no diagnostic.\nThree rows would have gone GREEN while measuring *unmapped memory returns 0*.  The repair moved\neverything below 64 MB **and added `assert max(P_CODE, P_PTRV, P_PCB_A, P_PCB_B) < 64*1024*1024`**,\nso the class cannot be reintroduced silently.  `swpctx mapped` then pins the same property at run\ntime: a `0` readback is reported as a SETUP FAILURE, not as a result.\n\n> **THAT IS THE THIRD DISTINCT ROW-CLASS THIS SESSION WHOSE WHOLE PURPOSE IS TO SEPARATE 'MEASURED\n> NOTHING' FROM 'MEASURED A ZERO'** -- the UBSan in-band control, the kill-table NUL check, and this.\n> They were built independently and they are all the same rule: *an apparatus that cannot fail\n> loudly will fail quietly and be believed.*\n\n**TEST-FIRST IS SATISFIED:** the witness shows the symptom on the committed build BEFORE any edit,\nwhich is what licenses a fix.  The probe exits non-zero on a red row, so `rc=1` here is the correct\noutcome and not an error.\n\n**AND IT UNBLOCKS [[alphapfnoffset]]**, which is the reason this row was worked first: that row's\nwitness needs a non-zero `apcb_ptbr` and a valid page table, and *this* defect clobbers `apcb_ptbr`\nwith the flags word -- so any translation probe built before this was fixed could not attribute a\nbad translation to the walk rather than to the swap.  The confound is now measured rather than\nsuspected.\n\n*** A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THIS ROW DOES NOT RECORD, found by COUNTING the assignments rather than\nreading them.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nOver `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:274-292`, the load block's field assignments are:\n\n    apcb_ksp 1   apcb_usp 1   apcb_cpc 1   apcb_asn 1   apcb_unique 1\n    apcb_decrsv0 1   apcb_decrsv1 1\n    apcb_ptbr  **2**        <- :278 = new_ptbr, :282 = new_flags\n    apcb_flags **0**        <- never assigned at all\n\n**So `:282` is a field-name transposition: it should be `apcb_flags = new_flags`.**  The value IS\nloaded -- the SAVE block stores `apcb_flags` to `ctx + 40` and the loader reads `new_flags` from\n`ctx + 40` -- and then lands in the wrong field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE 1 is the clobber the witness measures: `apcb_ptbr` ends up holding the flags word.**\n> **CONSEQUENCE 2 is new: `apcb_flags` is NEVER LOADED, so a context switch silently drops the\n> incoming context's flags.**  The row's title names only the first.\n\n*And the block's own comment at `:275` reads **\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"** --\nwhich is exactly the property it fails to have.  A comment asserting completeness directly above an\nincomplete list is the shape this project keeps finding: the claim is the thing least likely to be\nre-checked, because it reads as a description of the code below it.*\n\n**The counting is what found it.**  Reading the block, `:282` looks like an ordinary assignment in a\ncolumn of ordinary assignments; the eye supplies the field name it expects.  A `grep -o 'pcb\\.apcb_[a-z0-9]*\n*=' | sort | uniq -c` over the block makes the 2-and-0 unmissable -- **a census of a nine-line block,\nwhich is not where anyone thinks to run one.**\n\n*Fix design out to grok (`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/FIX_brief.md`) before any edit: whether\none token repairs both consequences, what reads `apcb_flags` today and might rely on it being stale,\nand whether the witness then goes 9/9 or only 8/9.*\n\n*** THE GROK FIX REVIEW RATIFIED THE ONE-TOKEN EDIT AND REFUSED TO RATIFY IT AS THE ROUND --\nbecause the witness was BLIND to the second consequence.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n*\"Both red rows read the same `got': A+16.  A build that only stops clobbering PTBR -- delete\n`:282', or assign new_flags to any other field -- also scores 9/9.  Consequence 2 is unpinned.\"*\n\n**That is a clause-(d) gap in my own witness**, and the repair it named is one readback on a page the\nprobe had already proved was backed.  Added as `swpctx flags' (PCB_A + 40) and MEASURED:\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111       FAIL   the clobber\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222   FAIL   the clobber's signature\n    swpctx flags     0x0          0xbbbb2222       FAIL   apcb_flags NEVER LOADED\n    HEAD 7/9 -> 7/10\n\n**Three red rows now pin three distinct consequences instead of one cell read twice.**\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, and a THIRD consequence found:**\n  * `apcb_flags` IS written globally -- by `wrfen` at `:221`.  My \"never assigned at all\" is true\n    only inside the load block, and I had stated it without that scope.\n  * *\"whatever the previous context left\"* is wrong: `swpctx` has never loaded that field from any\n    PCB, so the in-cpu value is **last `wrfen`, or 0 from the zeroed cpu struct**.\n  * **THIRD CONSEQUENCE:** today the SAVE at `:243-244` publishes that never-loaded word into the\n    OUTGOING PCB -- so a context switch *wipes the flags the guest planted*.  The bug propagates\n    outward into guest memory, not just inward into the cpu struct.\n\n**Every `apcb_flags` site in `src/` was enumerated and none relies on the stale value** (`wrfen`\nwrites without reading; the SAVE publishes; the header constants and `ALPHA_IF_CODE_FEN` are unused;\n`memory_alpha.c:46` reads `apcb_ptbr`, not flags).  *Sticky-FEN-across-`swpctx` is a PAL bug, not a\nbehaviour a reader is entitled to.*  **A separate finding volunteered in passing: `PAL_clrfen`\n(`0xae`) is named and user-mode-whitelisted but has NO `case`, so it reaches the default arm and\nstops the CPU.**\n\n*** `#507` APPLIED.  THE CENSUS THAT FOUND THE DEFECT IS THE CENSUS THAT CONFIRMS THE FIX. ***\n\n    before   apcb_ptbr 2   apcb_flags 0   (every other field 1)\n    after    apcb_ptbr 1   apcb_flags 1   (every field exactly 1)\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt, two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  *The witness re-run is in flight; its result is\nNOT recorded here until it lands, because a number written before the run that produces it cannot be\ntold from a number fitted to the answer.*\n\n*** `#507` MEASURED: 7/10 -> 10/10, SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx flags     0x0        -> 0xbbbb2222     <- consequence 2, pinned by the row grok demanded\n\nSix controls green on both runs.  Clause (i) discharged by the pair.  **Nothing committed** -- the\nFable hold stands and this waits as finished work rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n                                   contains the `#507' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*  #507.  One-token destination fix, `apcb_flags = new_flags' -- VERIFIED LIVE AT HEAD; census apcb_ptbr 2->1, apcb_flags 0->1.  A third red row (`swpctx flags') exists because grok REFUSED to ratify a witness that read A+16 twice -- deleting :282 also scored 9/9.  NO KILL TABLE, CLASS_WITNESS, ungated (`wiringcliff`).  RESIDUAL FILED NOT FIXED: PAL_clrfen (0xae) is whitelisted with no `case' -- that is a NEW QUEUE ID, not a hold here.  Codex's a0-vs-v0 split (`alphaswpctxreg', #514) already separated the second change under the same reopening rule, so combining across files would REVERSE a ruling this project accepted. CLOSED 2026-09-17 on the grok triage (regress/artifacts/<row>/ and _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_inwork.txt), which REFUTED THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISES: I asked about four rows with `commits` EMPTY and `sev: None`.  BOTH WERE MY QUERY BUGS -- I read `r.get(\"severity\")` when the field is `sev` (a wrong key and an absent value render IDENTICALLY), and I never queried `commits` at all, carrying `no commit yet` from a table that was true on 2026-09-07 and stale from 2026-09-08.  All four have SHAs; all four have severities.  The only real finding was that `state` was left `in work` after attribution was restored -- a lingering record, which is the resume-file rule applied to the ledger. NOT COMBINABLE.  The reopening rule was applied pairwise and every pair fails it: different files, different semantic classes, different detectors and oracles.  The shared `cpu_alpha_palcode.c, memory_alpha.c` file column was a CORRELATE -- the seat-audit derived it from the COMMIT's first src/ paths, and 7a41b3f is titled \"#505 #506 #507 #508\" and touches ~190 files.  `sgirergbroundtrip` is the control: same SHA, same bogus file column, an SGI RGB defect in dev_sgi_re.c, already closed as its own round.  More witnesses make more ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *\"I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet\"* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open \"on packet-only review\", which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file's last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *\"row 9's 'not applied' and 'unproven' descriptions are stale locally\"* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *\"an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.\"* *** THE BRIEF'S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *\"named and deliberately not applied\"*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5's LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *\"'#481's rows' is insufficient\"*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek's HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *\"edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.\"*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *\"combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does\"* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *\"these are coverage predictions, not newly run mutants.\"*  *** L4 -- I RECORDED THE BRIEF'S OWN HEADINGS AS CODEX'S ANSWER ON ALL NINE ROWS.  Found by the Fable seat, corrected 2026-09-17. ***  My extraction matched the first line containing the row id and a word from `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` -- and **`CLOSE` matched the word `closed` inside the brief's own heading**, which codex echoes.  So nine cells read `### N. <id> (SEV, closed) -- <title>`: the packet, handed back as the seat's verdict. *** IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW AND FABLE SAID WHY: \"an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.\" ***  The blank that would have been caught was filled with something that looks like an answer. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* Codex's real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY 'SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS' ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET'S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  My own brief's S0 told the seats *\"an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction\"* -- which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, *discharged by naming the file* -- row 2's condition (c), row 8's figures, deepseek's rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *\"the file-reading seats verified it\" is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5's arithmetic, row 5's unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** glm's *\"nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire\"* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** **THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex's row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into \"the file-reading seats said CLOSE\".",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43031:   "note": "\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase research -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  THIRD independent confirmation of the CONTENT rule, and the first scored by the POSITION-keyed detector shipped the day before.  sgirandomcolour/codex_scoped_20260908.err is a genuine CONTENT-FILTER REFUSAL: two markers IN THE TAIL, zero verdict blocks, the seat's own words being 'I can assess the code path and disposition, but I can't provide or run a guest sequence designed to reproduce the suspected defect.'  Counting the string would have been ambiguous here; position is not.  The routing fact holds: ask codex for the GAP, never for the WITNESS.  Measured the same day AGAINST a control: a GAP-shaped, four-file, premise-as-question brief (wiringcliff) returned a full 12.9 KB verdict from the same seat in the same session.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase research -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  A THIRD CODEX RULE, measured: the CONTENT rule (ask for the GAP, not the EDIT) is necessary and not sufficient -- an analysis-shaped brief was refused because the DIFF IT INLINED carried memory-safety language in its comments.  Two sibling briefs fired the same minute with the same shape and no such diff text (pcc2c040kill completeness; armbfxwidth32, whose diff is arithmetic) both answered in full.  RULE: for codex, a review of a memory-safety fix is pointed at the FILE (the ARGV_CAP pointer form) rather than inlined, or the diff is summarised -- and any brief that inlines a diff is scanned for the trigger vocabulary before firing.  The position-keyed detector caught it correctly; the SIZE (29 KB) would have read as an answer.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-10, phase research -- in `note` rather than in `entries` because the dashboard declares no main-loop seat, following this row's own 2026-09-09 precedent]\n\n*** A ZERO-BYTE CODEX ANSWER HAS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT CAUSE, AND THIS ROW'S OWN DETECTOR WOULD MISATTRIBUTE IT TO THE FILTER. ***\n\nTwo farmed briefs came back with 0-byte .txt files today.  The position-keyed detector this row shipped says REFUSAL, and for one of them it is right.  For the other the cause is in OUR LAUNCHER:\n\n    UnicodeDecodeError: 'charmap' codec can't decode byte 0x90 in position 7509\n\nThe inline launcher read codex stdout in TEXT mode, which on Windows decodes cp1252, and codex emits bytes cp1252 cannot map.  The wrapper died and wrote nothing.  **The carrier already records this exact trap for the Ollama seats -- \"write responses to utf-8 files, never print() (cp1252 crash)\" -- and it was never applied to the codex farm.**\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN AN ORDINARY BUG: a 0-byte answer is the SAME OBSERVABLE for four different states -- terminal refusal, quota wall, run still in flight, and now wrapper crash.  A project that has just built a refusal detector will confidently read the fourth as the first.  That is the seat-failure-misattributed shape rather than the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape, and it is harder to see because the detector AGREES with the wrong answer.\n\nFIXED at the source rather than in prose: the farm launcher now captures stdout as BYTES and decodes utf-8 with errors=\"replace\", so no decode can throw and the answer survives whatever codex emits.\n\n*** AND THE POSITION DISCRIMINATOR GOT ITS CLEANEST CONTROL PAIR TODAY, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. ***  The rule is that POSITION decides and COUNT does not, and here are two runs on the same day, same CLI, same effort:\n\n    codex_fixdesign_20260910   3 flags at lines 2345/2540/2587 of 3987  (59-65% depth)  ->  10,731-byte ANSWER\n    codex_unrowed_20260910     8 flags, the LAST TWO at lines 18860-18861 of 18863      ->  0-byte, TERMINAL\n\n**A `grep -c` scores the healthy run as the WORSE refusal** -- 3 flags against 8 is the wrong way round only if you count, and the run with FEWER flags is the one that died.  Mid-stream flags are individual tool calls that codex retries around and they cost nothing; a flag in the last lines is the seat refusing.  This is the same POSITION-versus-SIZE discriminator seat_verdicts.py already uses to catch a wall in an otherwise healthy-looking 36 KB answer, arrived at independently for a different transport.\n\nONE MORE DATA POINT ON THE CONTENT RULE, and it is a NEGATIVE one worth recording: **the refused brief was ALREADY the benign one.**  brief_unrowed.md is written throughout in ordinary-defect-triage language (\"Ordinary defect triage on an emulator\"), with no offensive-security wording anywhere, and it was refused anyway.  **So benign FRAMING is not sufficient**, and the trigger is closer to specific vocabulary than to tone -- which sharpens this row's title claim rather than merely confirming it.\n\nSTATED AS OPEN RATHER THAN CLOSED, because the controlling measurement HAS NOT FINISHED: brief_unrowed2.md is a second rewrite of the same question that additionally drops the phrase \"guest-reachable exits\" and every row-id resembling a CVE handle, and reframes it as \"library code that calls exit() instead of returning\".  At the time of writing that run is STILL IN FLIGHT -- 5 flags, all mid-stream, no terminal pair, no \"tokens used\" line, codex.exe alive.  **If it lands with an answer that is the controlled A/B this row has never had** (same question, same seat, same day, vocabulary the only variable).  If it refuses in the tail, the vocabulary hypothesis is refuted and the trigger is the SUBJECT.  Either way the result belongs here; it is not being predicted.\n"
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43077:   "title": "wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py -- a single-leg mode plus a pass/fail contract for the ONE WDC two-leg differential that is genuinely infrastructure-blocked: it imports none of wdc_altoffset_probe, carries its own pty/Emu, ties its expectations to DISK CONTENT (needs a POISONED image the battery does not build), and adds an endianness axis (-e maltabe).  RETITLED 2026-09-09 from 'all four WDC differentials': the other three are one instrument (`import wdc_altoffset_probe as W') and belong to `wdcaltoffleg'.  Its wiringcliff exemption is re-dated naming THIS row and DELETED in the same edit as its wiring when this ships.",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43078:   "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 by the wiringcliff ruling.  The four EXEMPT entries are to be re-dated NAMING THIS ROW, so the debt names its round instead of disappearing.  RETITLED 2026-09-09 after the Opus seat measured the 3 + 1 split (wiringcliff assess panel)."
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43097:   "title": "The THREE altoffset WDC differentials as ONE instrument -- wdc_altoffset_probe.py and the two that `import wdc_altoffset_probe as W' (_sig, _4bit): one module, one rig (-E evbmips -e malta, the battery's own _images/wdc-scratch-64m.img), one media family (the `c' in ciR: is a PREFIX -- MEASURED, the same image attaches as an IDE CD-ROM).  OWED: (a) the parent's `ok' -- already a complete verdict over 18 explicit expectations -- gets an exit status, FIRST QUESTION being whether a nonzero exit breaks the two-leg driver; (b) _4bit's single-leg form is an ORACLE CHOICE (its verdict is definitionally between-leg -- 'its whole point is that it DIFFERS by leg', :216), so the round chooses the fix-side absolute and says so; (c) _sig's `sig' oracle is NON-DISCRIMINATING (unreadable and unrelated values both read as 'declined') and is REDESIGNED, not bolted onto.  Each of the three wiringcliff exemptions is re-dated naming THIS row and DELETED in the same edit as its wiring.",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43098:   "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 by the wiringcliff assess ruling (rev 2), from the Opus seat's measured 3 + 1 split."
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43108:   "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 from the Opus seat on the wiringcliff assess panel; the loop concedes it created an instance the same day.  Codex design pre-read landed 2026-09-09 (regress/artifacts/witnessclassdoor/); the round's brief starts from it.",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46586:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Found by the GPT-6 Astra seat under the found-something-else licence while answering a tree-wide SHIFT-UB census -- it says so itself: *'one definite additional finding deserves its own correctness row ... This is CRITICAL and EASY, independently of the shift-UB census.'*  **VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the handler before filing.**\n\n*** THE DEFECT, and it is four lines you can hold in your head. ***\n    uint64_t tmp = cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[rs], tmp2;\n    while (sh-- != 0) { ... rotate tmp left by one ... }\n    tmp2 = 0;\n    while (mb <= 63) {\n            tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-mb));      <-- the MASK goes into tmp\n            mb ++;\n    }\n    cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] = tmp & tmp2;             <-- tmp2 is STILL 0\n`tmp2' is assigned 0 and never assigned again, so **the destination register is set to zero on every execution, for every operand, every rotate amount and every mask begin.**  The `|=' also corrupts the rotated value it lands in, but that is moot: the result is ANDed with zero either way.\n\n*** THE SIBLING TWO FUNCTIONS DOWN SHOWS THE INTENDED SHAPE, which is what makes the diagnosis certain rather than inferred. ***  X(rldicr) does the same rotate and then CLEARS bits in `tmp' (`tmp &= ~((uint64_t)1 << (63-me))'), assigning `tmp' directly.  X(rldicl) was written the other way round -- BUILD a mask, then AND -- and the mask was accumulated into the wrong variable.  The fix is one character: `tmp |=' becomes `tmp2 |='.\n\n*** WHY THIS IS THE MOST SERIOUS THING IN TODAY'S BATCH. ***  `rldicl' is not an obscure corner: it is THE canonical 64-bit PowerPC masking and zero-extension instruction, emitted constantly by compilers (`rldicl rA,rS,0,32' is the standard 32-bit zero-extend).  Every other defect filed today needs a guest to touch a particular register or width; this one is wrong on the ordinary path, for ordinary code, on every execution.\n\nREACHABILITY IS WIRED AND CHECKED: `case PPC_30_RLDICL: ic->f = instr(rldicl); break;' in the primary decoder, reached whenever `cpu->cd.ppc.bits == 64' -- the 32-bit path deliberately goes to `bad' instead.  So it is live on every 64-bit PowerPC CPU model this tree offers, and dead on the 32-bit ones.\n\n*** AND IT IS NOT A SHIFT DEFECT, which matters for how the round is scoped: the census explicitly records the computed count here as SAFE (mask counts 0..63, rotate counts 1..31, form NONE). *** This is a plain variable mix-up that the census found while looking at something else.  It does NOT belong to [[shiftubcensus]] or [[mipsextshift]]; it is its own correctness round.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built.  The rung-3 attempt is owed FIRST and looks unusually cheap: any 64-bit PowerPC machine, one `rldicl' with a nonzero source, and a register readback -- the wanted value is anything but zero, and the pre-fix build must show zero.  A guest-visible wrong RESULT is a far easier oracle than the process-death oracles most of this queue needs.  **Check first whether the existing PPC gates already execute one and pass anyway**, because a green gate over an always-zero instruction would itself be a finding about the gates.\n\n*** WITNESSED AT RUNG 3, 2026-09-10, AND MY FILING UNDERSTATED IT IN THREE WAYS. ***\n\n(a) **TWO TOKENS ARE WRONG, NOT ONE, AND THE OBVIOUS REPAIR WAS BUILT AND MEASURED STILL WRONG.**  The mask is ORed into `tmp', the ROTATED VALUE, so even setting the AND aside the data is destroyed by having every mask bit forced to 1.  `tmp2' never being written is a CONSEQUENCE of the same slip rather than an independent bug.  The seat built both candidates: the one-character `tmp |=' -> `tmp2 |=' measures CORRECT at 0 of 190 wrong, while DELETING the `& tmp2' -- the edit a future reader is most likely to reach for -- ships an OR where the architecture says AND and gets 126 of 190 cells wrong.  **It is worse than the present bug in one specific way: it removes the always-zero signature that makes the defect easy to spot while leaving the value wrong.**  Recorded so the next round does not take it.\n\n(b) **A SECOND OBSERVABLE CHANNEL: IT CORRUPTS CONTROL FLOW, NOT ONLY DATA.**  update_cr0 is fed the destination, which is always zero, so the record form reports EQ on EVERY execution regardless of operands -- measured 0x20000000 committed against 0x40000000 fixed.  Any branch on a masked compare takes the wrong arm.  A detector owes a row on this channel.\n\n(c) **IT IS REACHABLE ON A REAL MACHINE MODEL, not only a synthetic one.**  All three in-tree machines that select a 64-bit CPU return zero: bareppc, testppc, and macppc -e g5 -- the Power Mac G5, whose constructor gives it the PPC970.\n\nTHE ORACLE CAME FROM THE MANUAL, NOT FROM THIS TREE: the rldiclx RTL in ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt plus its prose, with field placement taken from the manual's own format diagram and then independently confirmed by inverting the handler's extraction expressions.  Deriving the wants from the sibling would have been weaker and from the handler itself circular.  196 of 196 encoded words round-tripped through THE TREE'S OWN DISASSEMBLER, a different code path from the execute handler -- the defence against this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap, and it matters here because a wrong register field yields zero and zero is the symptom under test.\n\nTHE CENSUS IS NON-VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, which is what makes 190 of 190 a real number: the source is all-ones, so the rotate is the identity and the architectural answer is exactly the mask, which is non-empty for every mask-begin in 0..63.  **And the seat measured a genuinely VACUOUS cell to show the boundary**: with a source whose selected window is all zeros the correct answer is itself zero and the row is GREEN on the broken build.  The rule for a detector author is to choose a source where every mask window is non-empty.\n\n*** THE GATE FINDING IS STRUCTURAL AND STRONGER THAN 'NO GATE COVERS IT'. ***  Both PowerPC gates drive macppc -e g4, an MPC7400, which the CPU table marks 32-BIT -- and the decoder sends every rld* to the bad path in 32-bit mode.  **So no gate has ever instantiated a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, and the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible to the gates BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by omission.**  That is better than a green gate passing over a broken instruction, which is what I had asked to be checked.  It is one coverage hole with one cause, and a single 64-bit probe closes all of it.\n\nSEVERITY CEILING, stated honestly rather than glossed: the only PowerPC kernel image in the tree is loaded onto a G4 and never executed, so nothing in-tree BOOTS a 64-bit PowerPC guest.  The defect is catastrophic for any such guest and there is not currently one, which is why the witness is rung 3 and why it cannot be rung 4.\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46602:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py (27 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. ***\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldiclzero]] witness's sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler rotates with a `while (sh-- != 0)' loop, which ALWAYS exits with sh == -1 because the post-decrement runs once more than the body.  The mask loop then terminates on a condition involving 63 - sh, i.e. 64 -- always.  The architecture requires the mask to END AT 63-SH (the rldimix RTL and its prose, '1 bits from bit MB through bit 63 - SH').\n\nMEASURED with the destination seeded to 0 and an all-ones source:\n    SH=8    got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffffffffff00\n    SH=32   got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffff00000000\n    SH=0    got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffffffffffff   -- the one safe case\n\n**This is the standard bitfield INSERT.**  A compiler using it to splice a field destroys everything to the right of that field.  Live for every SH != 0.\n\n*** THE DURABLE REPAIR IS NOT TO PATCH THE 63 - sh EXPRESSION. ***  That loop idiom is shared by rldicl, rldicr AND rldimi; rldicr survives only because it never reads sh again.  **Any future edit that reaches for sh after the rotate loop in any of the three will be wrong the same way.**  Save sh before the loop instead.\n\nSame reachability and the same gate blind spot as [[ppcrldiclzero]]: 64-bit CPUs only, and no gate instantiates one.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_design_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, 14,947 bytes, ZERO cyber flags): THE DEFECT PREMISE IS EXACT AND **MY PROPOSED REPAIR IS INSUFFICIENT**. ***\n\nThe post-decrement reading is confirmed exactly: the loop's final test evaluates the old value zero and then decrements to -1, **and this happens even for SH=0**, so the mask always covers architectural bits MB..63.  The rldimix RTL requires MASK(MB, 63-SH).\n\n**BUT 'SAVE sh BEFORE THE LOOP AND KEEP THE MASK LOOP' -- which I filed as the durable repair -- LEAVES TWO MORE DEFECTS.**  Both come from the manual's own mask definition, which makes BOTH endpoints inclusive and WRAPS through bit 63 to bit 0 when the beginning exceeds the end:\n  (1) the existing loop compares the INCREMENTED position against the inclusive endpoint, so where it terminates normally it **stops one bit early**;\n  (2) it **never wraps**, so other operand combinations run past bit 63 and evaluate invalid shift counts -- an undefined-behaviour defect hiding inside the wrong-value one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED REPAIR is therefore four coordinated changes rather than one: preserve the decoded `sh', rotate with a separate counter (`int n = sh; while (n-- != 0)'), advance the mask position modulo 64 (`m = (m + 1) & 63'), and terminate on the position AFTER the endpoint, modulo 64 (`m != ((64 - sh) & 63)').\n\n*A round that had taken my one-line framing would have shipped a fix that is still wrong at the endpoint and still undefined on the wrapping cases, and its detector -- built from the same framing -- would very likely have passed it.*\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  MY PREMISES HELD AND MY CHARACTERISATION OF THE DEFECT WAS HALF OF IT. ***\n\nCONFIRMED: `sh' is `-1' on exit for EVERY decoded SH including zero (the final test reads the old value, decrements, and exits), the rotate body IS a correct ROTL64, and the mask loop therefore always stops at `m == 64' and selects bits `MB..63'.  The architectural mask is `MASK(MB, 63-SH)', INCLUSIVE of both endpoints and WRAPPING -- RTL at `ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:29908' (`m <- MASK(b, ~n)'), endpoint prose at `:29909', mask definition at `:6879'.\n\n*** THE ROW'S TITLE DESCRIBES ONLY THE NON-WRAPPING HALF.  The defect has TWO directions: ***\n  * `MB <= 63-SH' (non-wrapping) -- it OVERWRITES the low SH destination bits that must survive;\n  * `MB > 63-SH' (wrapping) -- it OMITS the required insertion into positions `0..63-SH', leaving\n    those destination bits UNCHANGED.\nA repair tested only on non-wrapping encodings fixes one and leaves the other.\n\n**AND SAVING SH IS NOT ENOUGH -- THREE MORE EDITS ARE NEEDED, which is why my sketch was rejected.**  The comparison happens AFTER the increment, so the stopping position is the endpoint's SUCCESSOR, and the counter must be circular:\n\n        int m, n = sh;                       /*  1: keep SH  */\n        while (n-- != 0) { ... }             /*  2: consume the copy  */\n        do {\n                tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));\n                m = (m + 1) & 63;            /*  3: circular increment  */\n        } while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));     /*  4: successor, modulo 64  */\n\n**Changing only one of edits 3 and 4 does not repair the loop.**  A partial repair that saves SH and sets the bound to 24 at `MB=40, SH=40' while keeping `m ++' walks 40..63 and then enters the body with `m == 64', evaluating `(uint64_t)1 << -1'.  *** That is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR, and the seat declined to call it an infinite loop on the ground that doing so would assign DEFINED behaviour to an undefined program -- which is the correct discipline and is what my brief's question got wrong. ***\n\nWORKED CASE, usable as a row: `rldimi r4,r3,40,40' = instruction word **0x7864422e**, with `r3 = 0xffffffffffffffff' and `r4 = 0': the repaired loop visits 40..63 then 0..23, 48 iterations, mask **0xffffff0000ffffff**, which is also the correct result.  **The original handler produces 0x0000000000ffffff.**\n\nMUTANT, one character, defined result: `64` -> `63` in the repaired bound.  `rldimi r4,r3,0,0' = **0x7864000c**, `r3 = ~0', `r4 = 0' gives correct `0xffffffffffffffff' and mutant `0xfffffffffffffffe' -- it drops the inclusive endpoint, architectural bit 63.\n\nThe seat validated its replacement against an independent bit-membership oracle over **40,960 cases** (every SH/MB pair x ten source/destination pairs), max loop count 64.  **It could NOT compile or run the emulator** -- no native compiler, and WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED' -- so this is a READING plus an offline model, not a measurement of this tree.  A measuring seat still owes the build.\n\n**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32' is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is scored.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s two corrected helpers were verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `& 63`, the all-zero-mask assertion's normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY THE agy SEAT (owner-approved use of the measurement reserve).  WITNESS TEST-FIRST, FOUR-EDIT REPAIR APPLIED, KILL TABLE WITH **ZERO ESCAPES**. ***\n\n**THE WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME**, not asserted: `opus_ppc_rldimi_witness_20260911.py` and `witness_committed.out` at 23:02, `witness_fix.out` at 23:06, the repair after.\n\n    committed build (HEAD)   17/27   -- 10 failing rows, 431 of 495 census cells wrong\n    repaired build           27/27   -- 0 failing rows, 0 of 495 census cells wrong\n\n**THE REPAIR IS THE FOUR-EDIT FORM, BYTE-FOR-BYTE AS DESIGNED AND AS I VERIFIED IT OFFLINE OVER ALL 4096 (MB,SH) PAIRS** -- `int m, n = sh;` / `while (n-- != 0)` / `m = (m + 1) & 63;` / `while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));`.\n\n*** KILL TABLE, `killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt`, frozen instrument, one clone, sentinel dropped before mutant 1 and CLEARED ONLY AFTER BOTH CLEARANCE PARTS (source `cmp` CLEAN, binary FRESH): ***\n\n    BASE           27/27      M_NOKEEP_SH    16/27\n    M_ORIG         17/27      M_NOCIRC       19/27\n    M_MUT63        14/27      M_NO_MERGE     24/27\n    M_NOWRAP       21/27      HEAD           17/27\n    RESTORED       27/27\n\n**ZERO ESCAPES, AND EACH OF THE FOUR EDITS HAS ITS OWN MUTANT AND EACH IS KILLED** -- `M_NOKEEP_SH` for edits 1-2, `M_NOCIRC` for edit 3, `M_MUT63` and `M_NOWRAP` for edit 4.  *That is the sole-killer discipline applied to a repair's parts rather than to a detector's rows, and it is what makes \"four coordinated edits\" a measured claim rather than a description.*\n\n**TWO CONTROLS WORTH NAMING:** `HEAD` scores exactly `M_ORIG`'s 17/27 with the identical failing set, confirming the committed build IS the defect and the mutator reproduces it; and `M_NO_MERGE` reds ONLY the three preservation rows (`pres nowrap`, `pres wrap`, `pres sh0`), which is those rows earning their place.\n\n*** APPARATUS NOTE, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS REPORT NEARLY WENT UNREAD: agy's `--print-timeout 30m` EXPIRED WHILE ITS BACKGROUND TASKS WERE STILL RUNNING. ***  The print session returned 8.8 KB of progress narration and no report, with stderr reading *\"root agent idle; waiting for 2 background task(s)\"*.  **The report existed on disk the whole time** -- `opus_measure_20260911.md`, 13 KB -- and was found only by looking in the artifacts directory rather than at the seat's stdout.  *A build-plus-kill-table does not fit a 30-minute print budget; size the timeout to the job, and when an agy run ends with background tasks outstanding, GO AND READ THE ARTIFACTS.  Its stderr is the tell and it is not the documented 111-byte quota wall.*\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS: **pass 2 on the diff**, per the repair-chain rule -- one pass per floor-bound diff, closing on the first pass that finds zero breaches.\n\n*** PASS 2 (PARTIAL -- ONE SEAT, AND THE MISS IS NAMED): NO INCORRECT RESULT FOUND, ZERO FLOOR BREACHES, TWO CONCRETE CLAUSE-(e) ITEMS. ***  `ppcrldimimask/codex_pass2_20260911.txt`.\n\n**THE INERT-REPAIR CHECK PASSES, AND IT WAS THE ONE I MOST WANTED.**  Yesterday an adopted design in this same file turned out inert because a loop mutated the variable its guard tested before the guard ran.  Here the dataflow is `decode sh -> initialise n from sh -> decrement n during rotation -> read the ORIGINAL sh in the mask bound`, with **no intervening assignment, increment, decrement or address escape**.  The bound depends on the preserved `sh`, not the exhausted `n`.  **The repair is ACTIVE.**\n\n**TERMINATION IS PROVED FOR EVERY ENCODING, including the case I flagged.**  At `(MB,SH) = (0,0)` the bound is `0` and the mask loop runs **exactly 64 times** -- a full circuit, which is correct, because `MASK(0,63)` is all-ones and a `do/while` treats initial equality as a complete circuit rather than zero iterations.  Generally the loop runs 1..64 times, the increment stays in `0..63`, and no mask-bit shift has an invalid count.\n\n*** TWO CLAUSE-(e) ITEMS, BOTH CONCRETE, AND THE FIRST IS THE KIND A VALUE ORACLE CANNOT SEE BY CONSTRUCTION: ***\n  1. **A UB rewrite that produces every expected value.**  Replacing the rotation loop with `s = (s << sh) | (s >> (64 - sh));` invokes undefined behaviour at `SH = 0`, where the right shift has count 64 -- **and a compiler that recognises the idiom and emits a hardware rotate yields every correct test value anyway.**  *A value-checking instrument cannot distinguish correct output from undefined behaviour that happens to produce correct output.*\n  2. **Register aliasing.**  Computing the mask first and delaying the source load until after the destination is cleared preserves behaviour when `RA != RS` and breaks `RA == RS`.  That is the same aliasing axis named for the `rlwinm` work, and it applies here too.\n\n**AND A COVERAGE QUALIFICATION ON MY OWN TABLE:** `M_NOKEEP_SH` removes edits 1 and 2 TOGETHER, so that leg does **not** establish separate runtime kills for the two textual changes.  My claim that *\"each of the four edits has its own mutant\"* is therefore true of edits 3 and 4 and loose about 1 and 2.\n\n*** THIS IS A PARTIAL PASS AND I AM RECORDING IT AS ONE. ***  It was a SINGLE SEAT, fired because the measuring seat was mid-build on another round and a nine-seat panel would have contended with it.  The repair-chain rule closes a chain on the first pass finding zero breaches **provided the pass is valid -- roster complete or its misses named**.  The misses are named here: eight seats unfired.  **The round owes its full panel before it ships**, and the seat itself noted it was not given the probe rows and so cannot claim the instrument lacks alias checks.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c '^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11: CLAUSE (c) NOW GENUINELY DISCHARGED -- AN HONEST IDENTITY NULL MEDIUM, AND IT IS STRICTLY SHARPER THAN THE PRE-FIX LEG IT REPLACES. ***\n\n`killtable_nullmedium_20260911/` (sentinel `NULLMEDIUM_DONE`), built by agy 3.8 in `/tmp/gxrldimi_clone` against the frozen instrument:\n\n    NULL_MEDIUM (identity X(rldimi) body)   SCORE 13/27   -- FOURTEEN rows RED\n    RESTORED                                SCORE 27/27\n    Clearance: Part 1 (cmp vs SEC)=CLEAN    Part 2 (binary -nt source)=FRESH\n\nThe reds cover every substantive family -- `nowrap` x5, `wrap` x4, `pres` x3 -- **and both census rows (`census wrong`, `census zero`) also redden**, so they are not the pure bookkeeping I had predicted they might be.  *A prediction I wrote into the brief, refuted by the measurement, which is the right way round.*\n\n**THE COMPARISON IS THE FINDING, AND IT IS THE SECOND INSTANCE OF ONE PATTERN:**\n\n    ppcrldimimask   identity 13/27 (14 red)   pre-fix HEAD leg 17/27 (10 red)\n    ppcrlwimiwidth  identity 12/28 (16 red)   pre-fix HEAD leg 14/28 (14 red)\n\n**Both rounds, independently: the identity medium reddens MORE rows than the pre-fix medium the convention substitutes for it.**  That is what a sharper clause-(c) leg looks like, measured rather than argued -- and it is now carried to the flagship seat as question 7n-1, which asks whether *a strictly sharper form was available and cheap* converts an established house convention into a floor breach across the ~16 tables that use it.\n\n*The old discharge was `PRETABLE.out:69`, \"(c) null medium -> HEAD leg (whole-file pre-fix)\", with the PRETABLE itself predicting that leg identical to `M_ORIG` and the run confirming both at 17/27 -- two legs measuring one thing.  That sentence is superseded, not deleted: the leg's score was real; what was false was its NAME.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration is unverified, and the probe covers neither fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by argument:** independent in-memory models found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  *Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` IS CORRECT.**  Book I's RTL is `m <- MASK(b, !n)` with `n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n> *'There is no shared functioning detector here -- the permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.'*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`'s two expected-value helpers exist but implement DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound as a representation argument, not an accident**: assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the low-word insertion.\n\n> *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined to certify it** -- *'If a 64-bit processor's 32-bit execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high word, that remains wrong.'*  **Filed rather than guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46776:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py (28 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. ***\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldiclzero]] witness's sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler takes its operands as MODE_uint_t and then rotates with a shift pair using 32-sh.  cpu_ppc.c defines DYNTRANS_DUALMODE_32, so the file is compiled TWICE and MODE_uint_t is **uint64_t** in the 64-bit build.  The architecture specifies a 32-bit rotate over the LOW half of the source -- **the high half plays no part whatsoever** -- but the right-shift pulls bits out of the high half straight into the low 32 bits, which is exactly where the mask lives.\n\nMEASURED, source 0xAAAAAAAA0000000F, destination seeded 0x5A5A..., shift 4, full mask:\n    got 0x5a5a5a5aaaaaaaf0    want 0x5a5a5a5a000000f0\nThe 0xAAAAAA is high-half data the architecture says cannot reach there.\n\nATTRIBUTION IS PINNED BY TWO COMPANION ROWS: rlwinm on the SAME source and shift is CORRECT, because it casts to uint32_t before rotating, and rlwimi at SH=0 is correct because the ternary short-circuits the rotate entirely.  **So the defect is the missing truncation in this one handler, not the shape of the rotate expression.**\n\n*A row testing this is VACUOUS unless the source's two halves DIFFER: the seat's own first draft used an all-ones source, under which a 64-bit rotate and a true 32-bit one agree bit for bit, and it corrected the row rather than shipping it green.*\n\nADJACENT, NOT A DEFECT BUT WORTH RECORDING so a round does not rediscover it: rldic, rldcl and rldcr are NOT IMPLEMENTED AT ALL -- the decoder's default swallows them.  rldic in particular is documented and compiler-emitted.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: THE WIDTH PREMISE HOLDS AND **TRUNCATION ALONE IS NOT THE FIX**. ***\n\nCasting the source to `uint32_t' before the rotate is necessary and not sufficient.  The prescribed repair splits the combined declaration -- `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)' with the destination kept at the mode-dependent width -- **and additionally handles the WRAPPING mask**, because the manual's mask wraps when the beginning exceeds the end: `if (mb > me) ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);' AFTER the rotation and BEFORE the existing mask loop.\n\n*** AND A NEW FINDING THAT CONTRADICTS THE EARLIER SIBLING AUDIT: `rlwinm' AND `rlwnm' ARE NOT UNIVERSALLY CORRECT. ***  The witness measured them green on the cases it drove and the audit recorded them as correct; the design review says that is true of those cases and not of the handlers.  **A measured-green sample is not a proof of correctness**, which is this project's own reading-versus- measurement distinction cutting the other way for once -- the measurement was right about what it measured and the GENERALISATION from it was mine.  Re-check both before either is called clean, and do not let this row's fix be copied to them on the strength of the audit.\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND IT FOUND A SECOND DEFECT AT THIS SITE THAT I HAD NOT SEEN.  MY 'ONE-TOKEN CAST' PREMISE IS REFUTED. ***\n\nThe width contamination is confirmed exactly as filed: for `1 <= SH <= 31' the erroneous expression's low word is `correct_rot32(L, SH) | ((H << SH) & 0xffffffff)', and the existing low-word mask can select those bits.  `SH == 0' bypasses the shift expression and is clean.\n\n*** THE SECOND DEFECT: WHEN `MB > ME' THE ARCHITECTURAL MASK WRAPS ACROSS THE 64-BIT BOUNDARY AND COVERS THE ENTIRE HIGH HALF, so RA's high 32 bits must be REPLACED with the rotated word -- and this implementation preserves them unconditionally. ***  `ROTL32(x, y)' is defined as rotating `x || x', the low word DUPLICATED (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:17143'), and the rlwimi RTL at `:29989' combines that with `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)'.  The wrapping mask visits `MB+32..63, 0..ME+32' -- which includes every architectural position `0..31'.\n\n    MB <= ME   high 32 mask bits all ZERO  -> preserve old RA      (what the code does)\n    MB >  ME   high 32 mask bits all ONE   -> replace with rot word (what the code never does)\n\n**So the repair is narrowing AND a high-word insertion**, and it must sit after the rotation and before the loop mutates `mb':\n\n        uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(ic->arg[0]);\n        MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);\n        ...\n        tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;\n        /*  A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word.  */\n        if (mb > me)\n                ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);\n\nIn the 32-bit compilation the conversion back to `MODE_uint_t' discards that high half, so the existing behaviour there is unchanged.\n\nMUTANT, one character: `>' -> `<' in the new condition.  `rlwimi r4,r3,0,31,0' = **0x506407c0**, `r3 = 0xaaaaaaaa0000000f', `r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a' gives correct `0x0000000f5a5a5a5b' and mutant `0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5b' -- the low-word insertion succeeds and the high-word replacement is skipped.\n\nTERMINATION PROVED rather than assumed: both fields are masked `& 31', `mb' is reset to 0 on reaching 32, positions are `(b+k) mod 32', and ME is processed after `((ME-b+32) % 32) + 1' bodies -- between 1 and 32.  Every shift count `31-mb' is in range.\n\nROUND SCOPE: **TWO rounds, not one**, with [[ppcrldimimask]].  Same file and family, but one repairs operand lifetime and a circular inclusive traversal while the other repairs source width and a missing high-word insertion, and validating the second needs word-duplication and high-half expectations that lie outside the doubleword detector's contract.\n\nValidated offline over **655,360 cases** (every SH/MB/ME at both widths x ten operand pairs).  **Not compiled** -- the seat had no native compiler and WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED'.\n\n**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32' is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is scored.\n\n**THE HIGH-HALF INSERTION HALF IS CONFIRMED OWED, 2026-09-10.**  It was filed as depending on the [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] dispute; that dispute is now RESOLVED by PowerPC Book I v2.01, which states the wrapping MASK and makes no upper-half clearing claim.  So this round needs BOTH halves -- the source narrowing AND the `if (mb > me)` high-word insertion -- and the narrowing alone is not the instruction's repair.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s two corrected helpers were verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `& 63`, the all-zero-mask assertion's normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** CHECKED FOR THE INERT-REPAIR DEFECT THAT KILLED THE SIBLING DESIGN, 2026-09-11: THIS ONE IS SAFE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN LUCKY. ***\n\n`ppcrlwhighhalf`'s adopted repair turned out INERT because the DECODER mutates `mb` to equal `me` before the handler selection that tests `mb > me`.  The same question had to be asked here, since the proposed repair uses the identical guard.\n\n**IT IS FINE, and the difference is WHERE `mb` COMES FROM:**\n\n        X(rlwimi):  int mb = (iword >> 6) & 31;        <-- decoded FRESH, in the handler\n                    tmp = sh ? (rotate) : tmp;\n                    if (mb > me) ...                   <-- the proposed insertion, HERE\n                    for (;;) { ... mb ++; ... }        <-- mutation happens AFTER\n\n**`X(rlwimi)` does not consume a decoder-precomputed mask at all -- it decodes `mb` and `me` from `ic->arg[2]` itself -- so it never inherits the mutated value.**  `rlwinm` and `rlwnm` DO consume a precomputed mask, and their handler selection happens in the decoder, downstream of the loop that walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*So the two rounds are not two instances of one hazard: one repair sits upstream of its own mutation and the other sat downstream of someone else's.  Recording the reason rather than only the verdict, because a future round asking \"is my guard inert?\" needs the discriminator -- **does this code decode the value it tests, or receive it?** -- and not a list of which handlers happened to be safe.*\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY agy ON **GEMINI 3.8 FLASH (HIGH)** -- the first round on the 3.8 seat, switched and liveness-verified the same hour. ***\n\n**WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `witness_committed.out` 00:46, `candidate_fix.diff` 00:47, `witness_fix.out` 00:48.\n\n    committed build   14/28   -- 93 census cells differing from the architectural result\n    repaired build    28/28   -- 0 census cells differing, no failing rows\n\n**THE DIFF IS THE DESIGNED TWO-PART REPAIR, BOTH HALVES:** `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)` splitting the declaration so `ra` keeps `MODE_uint_t`, and the `if (mb > me)` high-word insertion placed AFTER the rotate and BEFORE the loop -- which is the placement that keeps it from being inert, since the loop walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*** AND IT FOLDED IN THE AXIS I HAD NOT ENUMERATED, WITHOUT BEING TOLD TO. ***  The pre-fix failing set includes **`alias nowrap`, `alias wrap`, `alias both`** -- REGISTER ALIASING, which a review named to me yesterday as an axis my four (mb, me, shift, compilation width) did not cover, and which I passed to this seat only as an open question.  **It rowed it.**  So `RA == RS` is measurably affected pre-fix and correct post-fix.\n\nThe rest of the pre-fix failing set spans both defects and both mask classes, which is what the brief demanded: three non-wrapping rows, four wrapping rows, `wrap fullword`, the two preservation rows, and the census.\n\n**STILL RUNNING when the print budget expired: the kill table** (`killtable_20260911/`).  Same orphan shape as this seat's earlier round today -- *\"root agent idle; waiting for 1 background task(s)\"* -- but the brief told it to write its report to disk as it goes, so the artifacts are accumulating rather than being held for a final flush.  **A 120-minute print budget was not enough either; the lesson is to collect from the artifacts directory, not from the seat's stdout.**\n\n*** THE KILL TABLE IS COMPLETE AND STRONG, AND ITS `NULL_MEDIUM` LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  Found by opening the mutator rather than reading the leg name. ***\n\n    BASE          28/28        M_ONLY_HIGH   19/28\n    M_REV_COND    13/28        M_POST_LOOP   19/28\n    M_ONLY_CAST   19/28        M_ORIG        14/28   (= HEAD, identical failing set)\n    HEAD          14/28        RESTORED      28/28\n    NULL_MEDIUM   28/28        <-- SEE BELOW\n\nClearance: **Part 1 (cmp vs SEC) CLEAN, Part 2 (binary -nt source) FRESH, sentinel cleared.**\n\n**THREE LEGS EARN THEIR PLACE AND TWO OF THEM ANSWER QUESTIONS I ASKED EXPLICITLY:**\n  * **`M_ONLY_CAST` 19/28 and `M_ONLY_HIGH` 19/28** -- each half of the repair ALONE is killed, so **neither half suffices** and the two-part claim is measured rather than asserted.\n  * *** `M_POST_LOOP` 19/28 -- the INERT-PLACEMENT mutant. *** Moving the conditional after the loop is KILLED, which is the proof-by-measurement I asked for that the placement is load-bearing.  **That is the exact defect that made the sibling `rlwinm` design inert, and here it is a dead mutant instead of a shipped mistake.**\n\n*** BUT `NULL_MEDIUM` IS NOT A NULL MEDIUM. ***  The mutator reads `if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"): replacement = FIXED_BLOCK` -- **so the leg applies the SAME fixed block as BASE and is BASE under another name.**  It scores 28/28 because it is identical, not because the detector survives a degenerate input.\n\n**VACUITY FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS THEREFORE UNDISCHARGED**: the detector must FAIL on a stale copy or a comment-only file, proving it carries identity rows and is actually testing the binary it claims.  A leg that cannot fail proves nothing and reads in the table as though it did -- *the check-that-cannot-fail shape, inside a floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch that shape.*\n\n**OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS:** a real null medium -- run the frozen probe against a STALE binary, or against a source whose only change is a comment -- and show it RED.  Cheap, one leg, and it is the difference between claiming clause (c) and discharging it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: THE KILL TABLE'S NULL-MEDIUM LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, SO VACUITY-FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS UNDISCHARGED.  L2 -- repaired in-round. ***\n\n`killtable_20260911/mutate_rlwimi.py:179` reads\n\n    if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"):\n        replacement = FIXED_BLOCK\n\nso the NULL_MEDIUM leg COMPILES BYTE-IDENTICAL CODE TO BASE.  Its 28/28 in `SCORES.txt` is not a result: all three legs are the same binary, and the leg would report 28/28 whether or not the detector could detect anything.\n\nClause (c) requires the detector to FAIL on a medium carrying none of the behaviour under test.  A leg that recompiles the fix is a third copy of the base leg wearing the clause's name.\n\n**This is the check-that-cannot-fail family arriving INSIDE the floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch vacuity** -- which is worth recording as its own shape.  The carrier's existing instances are a `say` in a shell script and a sentence in a seat table; this one is a kill-table leg, i.e. it sits in the measurement apparatus and renders as a scored row.  A reader of `SCORES.txt` sees four green identity-ish legs and reads three independent confirmations where there is one.\n\nNOTHING ELSE IN THE ROUND IS AFFECTED: the six real mutants each scored a genuine kill (M_REV_COND 13/28, M_ONLY_CAST 19/28, M_ONLY_HIGH 19/28, M_POST_LOOP 19/28, M_ORIG 14/28, HEAD 14/28), and M_POST_LOOP at 19/28 independently proves the conditional's PLACEMENT is load-bearing.  The fix is not in question; the FLOOR CLAIM is.\n\nIN FLIGHT: agy 3.8 is building the real leg -- an identity `X(rlwimi)` body, in a scratch clone, no shared build tree touched -- where every row that genuinely observes rlwimi must RED.  Any row still green under an identity body is vacuous by construction and is the most valuable thing that run can return.  Brief predicts the `census` bookkeeping rows may legitimately survive; that is to be DISCLOSED with its reason, not waived.\n\nSTILL OWED after that: the full panel pass 2 on the diff.  Held rather than fired because a measuring seat's kill table was in flight and panel seats are CPU-heavy -- a deliberate hold, neither a seat failure nor agreement.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c '^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration is unverified, and the probe covers neither fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by argument:** independent in-memory models found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  *Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` IS CORRECT.**  Book I's RTL is `m <- MASK(b, !n)` with `n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n> *'There is no shared functioning detector here -- the permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.'*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`'s two expected-value helpers exist but implement DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound as a representation argument, not an accident**: assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the low-word insertion.\n\n> *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined to certify it** -- *'If a 64-bit processor's 32-bit execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high word, that remains wrong.'*  **Filed rather than guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47141:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldimimask]] design review, which raised it as a limitation on its own repairs rather than as the thing it was asked about.  **Verified locally by opening the function.**\n\n`update_cr0()' branches on `if (cpu->cd.ppc.bits == 64)' and compares `(int64_t)value', else `(int32_t)value'.  `cpu->cd.ppc.bits' is a property of the configured CPU MODEL and does not change while the guest runs.  The manual requires the comparison to follow the current MODE (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:6357'), which on a 64-bit implementation is selected by the MSR SF bit and CAN change at run time.\n\nSo on a 64-bit CPU executing in 32-bit mode, a record-form result whose low word is negative but whose full 64-bit value is positive records GT where the architecture requires LT.\n\n**SCOPE IS THE WHOLE RECORD FORM, NOT ONE INSTRUCTION**: every `_dot' handler in the tree routes through this one function, which is why it is filed on its own rather than inside either PowerPC rotate row.  The seat's own statement of the consequence is the honest bound to carry forward: *those repairs do not establish correct record-form behaviour on a 64-bit CPU operating with SF=0.*\n\nLOW because reachability is unestablished in the sharpest way: it needs a 64-bit PowerPC CPU running with SF clear, and this tree has no gate that executes ANY 64-bit PowerPC instruction and checks the result -- see [[wiringcliff]].  A round here owes the mode plumbing first, and should check whether `cpu->cd.ppc.bits' is consulted this way anywhere else.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **\"EVERY `_dot` HANDLER IN THE TREE ROUTES THROUGH THIS ONE FUNCTION\" IS FALSE**, and that sentence is this row's stated warrant for filing separately (\"SCOPE IS THE WHOLE RECORD FORM\"). ***\n\n`cpu_ppc_instr.c:205-206`:\n\n    #define FDOT(n) X(n ## _dot) { CHECK_FOR_FPU_EXCEPTION; \\\n            instr(n)(cpu,ic); update_cr1(cpu); }\n\n`grep -c '^FDOT('` = **25 `_dot` handlers routing through `update_cr1()`**, not `update_cr0()`.  The real population is the 33 `DOT0`/`DOT1`/`DOT2` uses plus 4 explicit `update_cr0` call sites.\n\n**The CODE is right** -- floating-point record forms SHOULD set CR1 -- and the defect itself is unaffected.  What is wrong is the SCOPE sentence, and it is wrong in the expensive direction: *a round scoping its work from that sentence would sweep in 25 handlers the defect cannot touch.*",
e483d931:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49907:   "state_note": "*** STATUS 2026-09-25 (GXEMUL): #550 DISPERSES the block -- ten dates, 2026-10-08 to 2026-12-03, each with its reason (regress/artifacts/exemptredate_20260925/).  CLOSED. ***\n\n*** STATUS 2026-09-18 (GXEMUL): #536 SHIPS under `wiringcliff`.  Two probes wired (mk48_nvram, hpcmips_ctor); the other ELEVEN re-dated to 2026-09-27 BY OWNER RULING -- one block again, valid on the 27th, RED from 2026-09-28.  That is the cliff this row names, re-created on purpose; the row stays open until it is dispersed or discharged. ***\n\n*** ELEVEN PROBE EXEMPTIONS FALL DUE ON THE SAME DAY, 2026-09-20 -- THREE DAYS FROM THIS FILING.  Measured 2026-09-17 by the Opus 5 seat and verified here. ***  A date histogram over `check_probe_wiring.py`'s `EXEMPT` table: **11 entries dated `2026-09-20`**, plus one `2026-09-19` and one `2026-09-21` -- so **thirteen exemptions expire inside three days**, eleven of them together. *** AND THE PILE-UP IS AN ACCIDENT OF A DELIBERATE CHOICE, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING. ***  The file's own comment explains the date: *\"2026-09-20 matches the fbpending_bound:2026-09-20 deadline already standing in gate_offline.sh's SM_EXEMPT, so `#442`'s two debts fall due together.\"*  **Aligning TWO debts was a good idea; nine more have since been written to the same date, and nobody chose that.**  Each new exemption copied the date it found. **WHAT IT COSTS IF NOTHING IS DONE:** on 2026-09-20 the checker reds on eleven probes at once, and a gate that reds on eleven unrelated things is the shape this project already names -- *a rule that retro-fails at scale gets switched off, and a disabled rule is worse than no rule*.  The honest options are to WIRE them, to RE-DATE them individually with their own warrants, or to retire the ones whose rounds are closed -- but **not** to push the whole table forward as one edit, which is how the pile-up formed. *Note one of the eleven is `ppc_rldicl_probe.py`, whose repair is uncommitted and whose row (`ppcwitnessmaskoracle`) is open -- so that debt is about to come due on an oracle nothing executes.*",
